00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.693
of the last meeting.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.730
We'll come on to some updates
from board committees.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.420
We haven't had as many of
those just to be very clear.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.940
We are, because we've
got a number of new NEDs,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.940
we are moving into the
new committee structure

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.430
and hopefully at our next board meeting,

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.000
we'll have more updates
from the various committees.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.290
But we do have an update on
audit and risk from Jeremy.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.500
- Thank you, Penny. Just
quickly, we met last week.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.940
We are making good progress
on our risk register

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.390
and mitigations and increasingly
working with the department

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.140
to bring the risk register together

00:05:06.003 --> 00:05:08.340
and the same with the internal audit plan.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.970
We've got an update from
the internal auditors,

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.650
but also on how they're working together

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.833
with the DH internal audit team
to bring the plans together.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.250
We took an update from Gus Williamson

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.780
on the Primary Care
Support services systems,

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.520
identified a number of risks,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.330
and a paper on that topic is
going to come to the executive

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.150
to give it a bit more profile
and make sure we've got a plan

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.183
for transition over the
next two, three years.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.680
Really good progress from the
executive on open audit items.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.300
It's a bit of a canary in the coal mine

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.150
in terms of the overall culture.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.340
More to do there, and again,

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.720
an issue for the executive to look at,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.459
I think is to how the,

00:05:50.459 --> 00:05:52.809
particularly the priority
ones can be resolved.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:57.030
Good progress from the team
on getting the accounts out.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.530
It's quite a challenging situation

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.300
and the audit recurrence have changed,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.970
but good progress on the
NHS England accounts,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.000
and they should be coming before
the board in early October,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.300
and the provider accounts
hopefully shortly thereafter.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.700
And then finally we are about to move

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.410
on to a new accounting system.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.960
It's going to affect
the whole system, ISFE2.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.540
We're all holding hands together

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:19.860
and hoping it all goes well,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.890
but I think the team has done
a really good risk assessment,

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.860
contingency planning,
communication intersystem,

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.890
and well done to really
be involved in that.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.100
It is a mammoth effort.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.600
I think most of the team are in Leeds now,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.667
trying to make sure it goes okay.

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:35.313
So, well done to Elizabeth,
Vicky and the team for that.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.570
- Okay, thank you. Any questions, Paul?

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.570
- I was at the committee and
when we get the risk register,

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.880
I'd just really like to
draw everyone's attention

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.400
to what was one of the main risks

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.080
and the mitigation for that risk.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.390
One of the main risks is
we're too slow in bringing

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:59.390
about the change and so I
think one of the things we need

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:04.290
to keep in mind is whether
we are making that slower

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.990
or quicker because if
we're making it slower,

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.240
we make the risk greater.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.930
So the mitigation that we'll
be looking at through all

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.400
of our activities is are we
up for the pace of change?

00:07:19.937 --> 00:07:22.260
- So can I suggest that we come back

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:27.260
to that when we discuss
the 10-year plan update?

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:28.950
'Cause I think that's the moment

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.870
at which we should really be scrutinising.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.720
Are we doing enough? Are
we going quickly enough?

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:38.010
But is your question, Paul,
also to Jeremy and Elizabeth,

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.983
about are we capturing the
risk of that accurately?

00:07:42.334 --> 00:07:47.334
- I think we were in the
committee, in the risk committee.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.123
It's just that when you come out of that,

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.330
I think the conversation we had

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.560
is that most people's
experience of risk is change.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.773
And when we say the biggest
risk is not changing,

00:08:02.638 --> 00:08:05.730
that for a lot of people is
counterintuitive because,

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.520
and it's true, actually
it's true at the moment.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.080
The risk for the National Health Service

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.640
is not changing fast enough.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.157
I think most people experience
risk as changing too fast.

00:08:16.157 --> 00:08:17.800
- I think the point you made
in the meeting was the risk

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.750
is that in the system
in hospitals and GPs,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:22.680
the risk that we're planning

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.810
to take will be seen as being risky.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:27.540
And so we just need to be
careful as to how we communicate,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.040
how we get the message across,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.740
and how we deal with that
potential barrier to change

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.900
as we're trying to deliver
what we're trying to deliver.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:34.733
- Absolutely.

00:08:34.733 --> 00:08:35.790
So key point,

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.400
and let's say keep hold
of that for when we come

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.723
onto talking about it.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:41.453
Elizabeth.

00:08:41.453 --> 00:08:42.599
- Yeah, Penny.

00:08:42.599 --> 00:08:43.823
Just to echo something

00:08:43.823 --> 00:08:48.823
that's just been said really
and this ISFE2 systems.

00:08:49.108 --> 00:08:51.900
So it's a new globally system
for finance and accounting,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.540
and it's just a thank you actually to all

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.570
of the financial control
teams out in the wider NHS

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.755
because it's 42 ICBs, it's HSSIB,

00:09:00.755 --> 00:09:03.150
it's all the CSUs and it's NHS England,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:04.800
it's been a couple of years in the making,

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.300
and yesterday we made the decision

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.400
that we were ready to proceed

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.550
with a go live date effectively
of the 1st of October.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.920
So it's a huge amount of
work and a lot of people work

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.980
in long, long hours and over weekends.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.663
So again, to those financial control teams

00:09:18.663 --> 00:09:21.720
that are finishing accounts
and they're moving quickly

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.000
into this work, just a massive thank you,

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.210
'cause some of them are part
of a change process as well.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.540
- Okay, thank you. And
yes, we would echo that.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.650
Okay, so that's quite a
nice then link into update

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.710
from the digital and data committee,

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:40.620
digital, data, and technology committee.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.890
And Mark, perhaps you
might like to also reflect

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.540
on the point that Paul's raised,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.860
'cause this is one of the areas

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.920
where sometimes we look
at the risks associated

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.090
with adopting new technology,
but we don't look at the risks

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.300
associated with not adopting it, so.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.440
- Of course, Penny.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.903
So the DDAT met yesterday,

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.700
some really good progress in some areas.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.000
So progress on cybersecurity
across NHS England

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.150
and the resilience plan that
the board's been pushing

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.910
on for 18 months is good.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.000
There's clearly quite a
lot more work to be done

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.030
in the system and in
particular in the supply chain,

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.930
which I think will continue from here.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.800
I think the team working

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.480
on the single patient record
should be congratulated.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.500
Three options are now in
proof of concept build.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.193
So no longer in PowerPoint, but
actually in practising code.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.250
I think that brings home we need

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.300
to start doing a lot more work

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.520
on the engagement plan that goes with it,

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.330
'cause to your point about
things that need to be adopted

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.010
and will be changing for the whole system,

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.340
the single patient record is
a massive vehicle for change

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.553
and needs to be delivered.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.900
The NHS app look continues to
go from strength to strength.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:05.700
If you look at the adoption,
unique users, monthly logins,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.560
it's probably, and I need to check this,

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.333
the most used app in the country.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:16.310
There is absolutely clear use
cases for managing access,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:24.063
managing treatment paths,
and also driving prevention.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.180
I think the key task at the moment

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:31.380
is to select a small group
of really high impact cases

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.570
where we've got clear
clinical sponsorship,

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.420
which is something I'll come back to,

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:40.290
which can deliver both
improvements to patients

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.760
and productivity.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.020
And I think that list is there.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:44.730
I think we just need to
get to the shorter list

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:45.843
and start executing.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.590
The operating model
work for the combination

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:52.350
of the department, and
then HSE is going well,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.210
and I think we'll have
a substantive proposal

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.263
by the next meeting.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.450
I think if there's one theme

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.250
and it comes back to what Paul said,

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:06.240
consistently there seems
to be a lack of sort

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.430
of operational ownership

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:12.360
and vision driving the
technology team harder.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.450
And what I think that's
causing is us slowing down.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:19.710
And I think if you look at the need both

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:24.710
to reduce waiting lists to
improve performance in UEC,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.690
access to general practise,
improving prevention,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.100
technology is the answer,

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.140
and we need to drive
the adoption much faster

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.020
and the risk of not
adopting it is we continue

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.570
in the position we're in today.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.920
- Okay.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.950
Before we take questions, Ming,
anything you'd like to add?

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.170
- No, we had quite a long debate yesterday

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:48.170
on how can we align better and work better

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:51.630
in the matrix form between
the technology teams

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:55.140
and you know, the clinicians
and also the ops teams,

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.760
which we are starting to do.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.370
We've had some really good
engagement and workshops,

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:02.610
but it's a starting of the
journey rather than the finish.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:06.483
- Yeah. And on the single patient record.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.980
So, good to hear that progress,
Mark. What's your sense?

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:14.523
I mean, neither of you,
when will we have it?

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:17.603
- Which day?

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.320
- [Penny] I'm happy to go
with a quarter of a year.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:27.750
- We should have a prototype
ready for review kind

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:31.001
of end of the year, end
of this calendar year.

00:13:31.001 --> 00:13:31.834
- [Penny] Okay.

00:13:31.834 --> 00:13:34.290
- For adoption and further
development to be honest,

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.720
'cause we will then have down selected

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.193
to hopefully one pattern
that we want to deliver.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:46.020
We are not really going through
this as a, we will buy this.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:47.700
What we are really looking
at is what's the concept

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:50.490
we're trying to achieve
and what's the modularity,

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.470
how much of it have we already got?

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:53.577
What do we actually add to?

00:13:53.577 --> 00:13:56.220
And then how do we accelerate the change?

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.430
So in all this work,
as Mark said, you know,

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:03.210
we want to find the really great
use cases that we can drive

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:06.420
through and make sure that
it does all those things

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.100
'cause we're going to
iterate this over time.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.150
It's not going to be
one, you know, quick bang

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:11.200
and it's done.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.350
- Okay. All right, thank you.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.860
So I've got Robert, I've got Louise.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:18.930
So we'll take, and Suresh,

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.580
I'll take all three questions first,

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:22.650
and then come back to Mark's comment.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:23.860
- So it's more of a
comment than a question,

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:27.960
but I think as Mark said, I
think the progress is terrific.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.660
The point of these technologies

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:35.040
is to enable pathway redesign
and productivity gain.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.680
And I think the conclusion we came to,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.210
is that a great opportunity
to build these technologies,

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.730
for example, single patient
record into pathways

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.740
is the modern service framework work.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.750
So that's where you begin to see

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.600
how you can re-engineer a pathway,

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.813
a whole pathway using these technologies.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.920
- Yeah, we might come back to that,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.710
and talk about partly
modern service frameworks,

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.740
but also all clinical pathway redesign.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:05.700
Suresh.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:09.030
- I just wanted to add that
really productive session.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.130
My observation is that there is a lot

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:12.900
of measurement technology that's built

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:16.160
outside of the NHS
watches, wearables, rings,

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:18.300
pick your favourite.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.790
I think our vision should
be bolder and braver

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:23.430
to encompass what's happening
in the outside world

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:24.690
and bring it back in,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.890
especially as we are
focusing on prevention.

00:15:28.347 --> 00:15:29.801
- [Penny] Okay. Louise.

00:15:29.801 --> 00:15:30.634
- Yes, thank you.

00:15:30.634 --> 00:15:33.030
So it does absolutely seem
like fantastic progress

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:34.980
and we know that people really

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:37.320
on the whole welcome using the app.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:41.280
Can you tell us more about how
you've involved service users

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.530
in the development of it?

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.843
- Okay, so, go on,
Sarah-Jane, do you want to go?

00:15:46.843 --> 00:15:48.210
- Just to, totally,

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:49.980
'cause we've had a really
productive meeting, didn't we?

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:53.400
Mark, Ming, and I, about
what we might do on UEC here.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.100
I do think we need to
acknowledge that for many of us,

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:57.780
we don't really know
the art of the possible.

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.270
So if we are waiting for
the, I know we're not,

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.220
but if we were to wait for
the vision to just come

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.040
from current operational
and clinical leaders,

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:06.450
I'm not sure it's there.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:09.480
It's got to be the meeting
of the genesis of the art

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.640
of the possible with the tech teams

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:13.050
to really drive ourselves forward,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.800
'cause I think a lot of us
start to invent or start to work

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:18.600
on what was last year's technology,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.400
not the technology for
three to five years.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:23.340
So we need pushing on, so
we're all up for the challenge,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:25.010
but I don't think it's as simple

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:27.870
as we just have a clear
vision and then off we go,

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.193
and try to implement it.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:32.970
We need to be really pushed
I think by the tech people

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.880
in terms of what will be
possible in a few years time.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:37.890
- Okay, so I'll try and
weave all these together.

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.130
So there's essentially
some ways similar question

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:44.760
about how do we really get
tech embedded into all services

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.400
and do that both in a push and a pull.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.650
So we're not waiting as
your point, Sarah-Jane,

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:50.850
for staff to say,

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.100
it's about how do we say
these are good ideas,

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:54.483
let's start trying them.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:58.560
How do we really, Robert's point,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.930
adopt that into all of our care pathways,

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.870
whether the modern service
frameworks or role,

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:06.420
or the care pathway designs,
how do we involve users

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.030
as we're developing different products?

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:11.490
And then how do we open up the app

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:14.160
which was part of the 10-year
plan to be able to, for people

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:18.093
to put in their own data from
wearables and other devices?

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:22.383
Ming, or you can ask it for Mark.

00:17:23.252 --> 00:17:25.860
- So I think we should pick up,

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:27.390
let's deal with the user input,

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:30.453
'cause I think the app team are
one of the prime example of-

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:35.160
- So we actually have a
bank of about 25,000 people

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.890
that we test the
functionality of the app with,

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.280
and that's done on a regular
basis just as routine.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.370
Every kind of feature that
they change is actually tested

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:46.290
with that bank of users.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:47.700
So it's quite a lot of engagement.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.193
And then there's local engagement as well.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.810
- And then on the sponsorship,

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.580
I think in all periods of change,

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.290
you get these slight dislocations.

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:01.830
I think Sarah-Jane's point
about the meeting with UEC,

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.230
what the technology can do,
what the vision for UEC is,

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.240
I think we need to mirror that.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.780
I think similar work's been
done on outpatients and cancer.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.430
We need to do the same
thing on primary care,

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:15.840
but pulling the business
closer to technology

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.340
and technology closer to the business

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.680
and actually creating
this after the possible

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.600
and some pace I think is the key.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.470
And it's, I think collectively
everyone on the committee,

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:28.470
this is no longer a technology challenge,

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.270
this is a business change challenge.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:32.130
The tech can be done,

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.140
the progress made on single patient record

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:39.140
in six months has basically
proved we can do this.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.120
It's now a question of execution.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.257
- Okay, I think we might
need to come back to this

00:18:46.257 --> 00:18:49.410
'cause I think there is a bit
about how do we help the ante

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.960
on it or what's the
best way of doing that.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:53.520
So maybe we take that offline,

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:55.350
have a think about how to do that,

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:56.430
take some specific things.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.017
And then wearables into the app.

00:19:00.072 --> 00:19:03.030
- There are some experimentation
already being done

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.230
on wearables.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:07.320
I think again the standards
are there, you know,

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.290
we just need to decide
where we integrate it.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:13.200
Is it with a single patient
record? Is it into the app?

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.200
And then how do we hold that
data and how do we curate it?

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.480
So it is not, the technology's there,

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:19.920
it's the usage of that,

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.330
and how we want to use that
to change pathways really.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:27.450
- Okay. All right, good.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:28.283
To your bit, Louise.

00:19:28.283 --> 00:19:33.230
We should have a board meeting
up in Leeds sometime soon

00:19:34.112 --> 00:19:35.247
to make that happen.

00:19:35.247 --> 00:19:37.320
And there's a whole team there of people

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.800
who do some really cool stuff
with tech and you can go

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:45.600
and try it yourself and really
interesting particularly

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:48.603
about looking at people who
find it harder to use tech.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.760
So we could go on a board visit
and actually test out some

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:57.330
of the stuff that they're
doing live, a good thing to do.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:02.330
Alright, good. Let's
move on to performance.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:07.430
So we have a very detailed
performance report.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:11.370
This remains working. Oh
sorry, I jumped over Jim.

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:12.203
- That's fine.

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:15.043
You honestly skip past it.

00:20:15.887 --> 00:20:19.438
- Sorry, Jim, your update first.

00:20:19.438 --> 00:20:20.940
Thanks, David.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:22.140
- Just very briefly then.

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:25.800
So September's always,
it feels very chaotic.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.230
It's always very busy
in the national system.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:30.270
We've had a busy couple of weeks in terms

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:33.660
of public accountability,
public accounts committee,

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:35.580
health select committee,

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:37.500
stock takes with the prime minister,

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.110
various other things which
were all sort of scheduled,

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.920
but an important point of
the year when we're starting

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.450
to think about how we'll land this year,

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:47.610
how do we complete this year
as well as we should do,

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:50.520
how do we navigate the
tricky winter, et cetera.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:54.570
They've crystallised a few
things for us to really focus on,

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:56.460
which we're working on
over summer as well, which,

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:58.260
and we'll touch on it in a second.

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:00.450
Lots of progress in our operating model

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:03.750
and we've appointed chairs of cluster,

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:07.030
ICBs, chief execs soon
to be announced as well.

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.790
We do have a tension in all of this

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:10.770
about the voluntary redundancy scheme,

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.720
which has been well-publicized

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:16.980
and in a pretty long awkward conversation

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:18.450
with treasury colleagues about it all,

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:19.620
just trying to unlock that.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.650
And at some point in
the next couple of weeks

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.240
if we've not managed to land that,

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.580
we'll have to work through a plan B option

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.520
to allow our ICB colleagues to move on,

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:32.340
and similarly within NHS in NHS England,

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:33.720
but a lot of really good work there.

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:36.810
Also, the work led by Glen and colleagues

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.420
on the oversight framework, I
think it's really taken shape.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:43.080
It's that and the weekly
tables that are sent out,

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:46.650
et cetera, common discussion
points now in the service,

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.150
absolutely not fixed yet,

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:53.150
so still a lot of refinement
to do, but fantastic progress.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.730
Also through summer, which
we'll touch on later on,

00:21:56.730 --> 00:22:00.630
we've done a lot of work with
colleagues in the service

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:04.020
and across government on
bringing a 10-year plan to life.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.570
So what we're really
trying to do is we're set

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:08.280
out in the letter at the end of last week

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:11.340
is get the balance rate
between short term delivery

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.200
landing this year on our financial

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.833
and operational imperatives,
but start building the future.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:20.700
Going back to some of the
points raised earlier on,

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.490
there's a really difficult
balance we've got to strike

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:26.970
in all of this between the
being really ambitious,

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:29.550
but not making it break.

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:31.860
So in the same sentence,
we can talk to colleagues

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:35.160
who want more change, more rapid change,

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:37.290
but are saturated by the amount of change

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:38.310
that's already going on.

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:39.720
And that's just a live dynamic

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.483
that we're all trying
to manage well together.

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:47.280
It feels like a long time ago
since we had our last board

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:49.913
as well and if you remember
the main dominant issue

00:22:49.913 --> 00:22:52.380
in that board was industrial action.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:53.953
And I think, you know,

00:22:53.953 --> 00:22:57.570
I think it's fair to say the
service led by colleagues here,

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:58.740
but right throughout the service

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.280
did a fantastic job
managing a difficult set

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:05.700
of circumstances in really
minimising the amount

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:07.530
of disrupted activity.

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:09.590
That said, we are a little
bit off track on some

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:11.700
of our metrics as a consequence,

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:15.660
but really significant
progress over previous rounds

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:17.700
in terms of maintenance of activity levels

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.397
and there are a lot to learn
in that if we do have to go

00:23:21.397 --> 00:23:24.390
through it again.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.430
And then I'll come back to Mark's point

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.770
and challenge on the tech.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:32.280
In those process I've described,

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:36.270
the publicly accountability
sessions, et cetera,

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:37.350
Our work with the service,

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:40.890
great chief exec session last Tuesday.

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:43.803
There are two standout
things we have to unlock,

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:46.980
and technology is a part of the solution.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:51.450
Outpatients is the
thing we have to unlock.

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:55.200
We're just having another go
now about how we get into that,

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:58.740
but it is the thing that we
have to find a way forward on,

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:03.740
and also how we change our
service model to uncrowd our EDs.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:06.870
So I started the day yesterday

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:09.480
with a Society of Acute
Medicine in Manchester,

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:11.700
ended the day with
Penny, with the president

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.470
of the Royal College
of Emergency Medicine.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.710
Wherever you talk, whoever you talk to,

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.850
there's a lot of tension in
that system and technology,

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:23.790
new pathways is probably
a big part of the way out.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.550
So I would say if we're looking for things

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.410
where there's a really strong operational

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:31.260
and business imperative to
bring technology to life,

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:34.860
they are probably the two
things for us to focus on.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:36.930
And then just very finally
I just want to say,

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:38.700
we say this all the time
and it's really true.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.770
Last Tuesday session was really fantastic.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:43.560
We are trying to work with the service

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:46.839
alongside the service,
keep everybody with us,

00:24:46.839 --> 00:24:50.430
keep everybody as
motivated as they can be,

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:52.470
but realistic about what's possible.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:55.560
The leadership response right
through it throughout the NHS

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.448
so far this year has been
absolutely fantastic,

00:24:58.448 --> 00:25:03.090
and we as a board really
need to try and nurture that,

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:05.670
develop that and keep
it going through this,

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:06.990
what will be a tricky period.

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:09.390
Winter's always a tricky
period for the NHS.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:10.230
So I'll stop there, Penny.

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.353
Happy to take any
questions if there are any.

00:25:12.353 --> 00:25:14.283
- Great. Questions for Jim?

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.702
Yeah, stunned everyone.
- Back to David.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:25.380
- Just before that, just
because we've got a couple,

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:28.230
I think what we say a bit
more, Jim, about, you know,

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:31.020
that working with the chief exec community

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.840
'cause there are a few things
which really struck me.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:38.370
One is that you are including
GP leaders in that group,

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:40.803
which is really crucial, you know,

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:43.890
not something that's
happened historically,

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:45.780
but it's obviously a really important part

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:47.310
of the healthcare system.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:51.540
And the second bit is that you
are asking amongst that group

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:56.540
of the leadership group
essentially of the NHS to work

00:25:56.790 --> 00:26:01.380
with colleagues here to develop proposals,

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.000
whether it's for neighbourhood healthcare,

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.790
whether it's for outpatient
redesign, and so on.

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.490
So just say a bit more about that.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:10.050
- So I'll bring Glen in a second as well,

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:14.550
who's led a lot of this,
the stream work over summer.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:18.360
But we are absolutely
trying to work together

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:21.780
alongside each other rather
than in a hierarchical ways,

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:23.040
partly recognition.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:24.870
You can't run everything centrally,

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:29.013
this kind of size of
organisation or system.

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:32.610
And colleagues have really
stepped up with that.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:36.270
So whether that's
10-year plan work streams

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:39.060
and bringing IHOs to life,
neighbourhood care to life,

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:40.680
you know, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:41.820
We are also getting a lot

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.950
of people come forward
all the time with ideas,

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:46.770
suggestions about other
operational issues.

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:47.820
So wherever that happens,

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.370
we're just trying to
go where the energy is,

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:54.012
and keep people involved and, you know,

00:26:54.012 --> 00:26:56.520
there's some practising chief
execs in the room as well.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:58.770
It minimises the risk then of us,

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:00.540
thinking we're solving problem A,

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:03.690
it's actually problem B,
or they find a solution.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:04.940
When you see it on the other side,

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.140
it actually doesn't fix the problem.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:10.230
Partly recognition of,
it's just very complicated.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.373
There's an awful lot of
complexity out there,

00:27:12.373 --> 00:27:13.950
but that's really worked.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:17.040
I started the day with a
conversation with a chief exec

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:18.480
who told me quite clearly,

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:22.290
we're not going fast enough
when IHOs, which is good,

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:26.520
you know, so there's good
healthy challenge out there

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:29.310
and we've opened up an awful
lot of channels for all

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.430
of us to have those conversations.

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:36.533
- Okay, and well, just one thing,

00:27:37.581 --> 00:27:38.414
we're holding this in the public,

00:27:38.414 --> 00:27:41.130
IHO is Integrated
Healthcare Organisations.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:41.963
- Yeah.

00:27:41.963 --> 00:27:45.960
- Concept of saying that a single provider

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.420
could provide a broader range of services

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.240
across the acute spectrum,
including primary care,

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:52.073
community care.

00:27:52.073 --> 00:27:54.390
- Yeah, and taking whole population risk

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:55.533
for that population.
- Yeah.

00:27:55.533 --> 00:27:57.270
- Glen.

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:59.603
- I'm still practising
as a chief exec after...

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.460
- [Jim] You're getting better though.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:05.490
- Every day in every way.

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:07.803
The engagement has been great actually.

00:28:08.679 --> 00:28:10.590
And so we basically,
anyone who put their hands

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:12.180
up at the previous chief exec session,

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:13.500
we brought them in and it was great

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.213
to have the GP leaders there too.

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:17.430
And then also I don't think we're turning

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.380
down any other option to engage.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:22.920
So mental health chief execs
forum last week, et cetera.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.230
So everyone's really enthusiastic.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:28.230
They also like the fact
that we are going to them

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:31.530
with a rough set of principles

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:33.300
that they are enhancing and adding to.

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:36.150
It's not us giving an answer and engaging.

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:37.620
It's about them co-producing it,

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:40.740
and they really welcome
that and that will continue.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:41.763
- Great. Rob.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:43.761
- Thank you, chair.

00:28:43.761 --> 00:28:44.670
I think the leadership being shown

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:47.580
by the chief executive
community now replicated

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:48.630
in the other professions,

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.750
it's really starting to create a coherence

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:52.680
across the service.

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:55.440
So Elizabeth, I think you
brought the FDs together

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:57.360
for the first time in over six years.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.900
We've got the chief operating
officers coming together

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:01.733
in October.

00:29:01.733 --> 00:29:03.810
We're doing the same with the
communications professionals

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:06.810
so that coherence that's
been driven centrally

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:09.600
by the chief exec community,
the GP leadership,

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:10.950
it's kind of echoing in each

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:13.429
of the different professional areas.

00:29:13.429 --> 00:29:17.340
And that's I think creating
much more ambition out there

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:20.940
in the service instead
of waiting for a policy

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.910
or a strategy to tell us
how we need to do something.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:25.590
You've got a lot of folks coming forward

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:28.050
with really innovative ideas

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.012
that perhaps we haven't thought of that,

00:29:30.012 --> 00:29:32.520
then start to lead the way
in terms of what we're doing.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.500
So it has a multiplier effect

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.930
across the whole leadership community.

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:41.730
- Great, good. Right,
let's go on to performance.

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:44.340
So there's a lot in here.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:47.133
I think, presume everyone has read it.

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:50.434
- [David] 86 pages, we
can take you through.

00:29:50.434 --> 00:29:52.590
- No, thank you.

00:29:52.590 --> 00:29:54.900
And I know we've got
quite a lot of questions

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.300
and comments so, but do you
want to do a quick intro?

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:58.857
- Yeah, thank you very much, chair,

00:29:58.857 --> 00:30:01.770
and thank you colleagues
who have helped develop

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:05.130
this performance report,
colleagues around the table,

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:06.540
many of whom we've interacted with.

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:09.120
So just a brief reminder and
then I'll just perhaps touch

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:11.670
on some of the areas
of improved performance

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:12.510
and some of the areas

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:14.880
that perhaps a little bit more challenged,

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:16.980
noting as always that my
colleagues around the table

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.810
are the real experts
in each of these areas

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:21.180
in delivering some of these key metrics.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:22.200
So first and foremost,

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:25.890
we have adapted the structure
to focus on six key areas,

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:27.840
improving health, reducing inequalities,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:31.110
effectiveness and experience
of care, patient safety,

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:34.200
people and workforce, all
of the access targets.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:36.510
And finally, finance and productivity.

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:39.180
And you'll see different to the
last time we met as a board,

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:41.820
we focus particularly
with board advice on areas

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.520
such as primary care,
maternity, community,

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.760
cardiovascular, diabetes, and prevention.

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:50.250
So there are, and we'll
see in the next item,

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:51.870
but we can perhaps gather it in the same

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.030
where there are areas we
can look to improve on,

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:57.030
and develop these metrics
and we are keen to,

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:59.370
but noting as I just said,
there rather flippantly,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:00.930
we're at kind of 86 pages.

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:03.990
So the key thing is how do you adapt it?

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.820
How do you make sure
it feels real and live,

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:08.070
and we don't swamp people with paper?

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:08.997
The other thing the team are doing,

00:31:08.997 --> 00:31:10.860
and a big thank you to
the performance team,

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:12.900
have worked really hard to
pull this together is seeing

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:16.500
how we could use statistical
process control a bit better

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.840
so we can really look at
themes and trends in a way

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.360
that perhaps this report
doesn't necessarily pull

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:23.010
out at this stage.

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:24.360
So if I could just take
you through a couple

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:26.370
of areas particular of highlight

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:27.750
in terms of improved performance,

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:31.080
noting that most of the data
here completes the end of June

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:33.900
and in some cases where we
have complete lockdown data,

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:35.700
we have it in July as well.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:38.610
So breast cancer screening
obviously an important part

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:41.340
of our overall objective is returning back

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:44.250
to much higher levels from the pandemic.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:46.230
The number of inpatients who are autistic

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:48.570
or having learning disability is reducing,

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:50.850
which is something we will
pick up I'm sure later on,

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:52.830
but has been a big area of focus.

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:55.620
There are a number of key
metrics around general practise

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.990
with which the service
should be very proud.

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:00.270
Firstly, access to first appointment,

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:01.980
which continues to improve.

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:04.110
Secondly, the percentage of patients

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:07.650
who are describing booking
their appointments as easier

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:10.950
or easy up to nearly 75%,

00:32:10.950 --> 00:32:13.740
which is a record in terms of the levels

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.360
of performance we have.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:16.950
In urgent and emergency care,

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:19.110
which I'm sure Sarah-Jane
will pick up later on.

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:22.920
We've seen improvement continuing
in cat two response times,

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:25.534
which we know is really
relevant particularly

00:32:25.534 --> 00:32:27.930
as we enter the darker and colder months.

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:30.480
And finally in areas like
children and young people,

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:33.600
particularly with focus
on mental health services,

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:36.300
we are seeing a much increased improvement

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:38.220
with regards to access.

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:39.720
Community mental health more generally,

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:41.790
the access rate as well
continues to improve,

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:44.280
which I think matches the
commissioning intentions

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:47.490
and the good work that many
providers have put in place.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.860
So there's a lot more that
we should be very proud of,

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.657
and we should congratulate our
colleagues out in the service

00:32:51.657 --> 00:32:54.540
who are working really
hard in all of these areas.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:56.100
There are, however a number of areas

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.080
where performance remains challenging

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:00.900
and these remain our priorities of focus.

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.630
I'm sure Mark will pick up
particular areas of elective

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:04.860
and cancer should we raise,

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:07.950
but actually some really
good areas of improvement

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:11.130
in many of those particular areas.

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:13.620
But the percentage of
patients waiting over a year

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.110
for community services is
something we have focused

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:18.920
substantially on of late
because that isn't going

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:21.180
in the right direction at the moment.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:22.710
This is an area of priority

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:26.010
and in the early data that we
started to see most recently,

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:27.390
this is starting to change

00:33:27.390 --> 00:33:29.970
and improve again with the
way we commission work,

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:33.480
but this is an area of
significant concern.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.970
The percentage of patients waiting

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:40.020
with suspected autism over
13-week continues to be an area

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:42.930
of concern and running the
regional performance reviews,

00:33:42.930 --> 00:33:45.270
this is an issue in almost every region

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:48.480
and is a significant
area of priority for us.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:52.020
Cervical screening again
continues not significantly,

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:54.540
but is not perhaps making
a stead way forward

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:56.160
that one would hope.

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:57.510
Percentage of mental health beds

00:33:57.510 --> 00:34:00.330
that are out of area continue
to concern all of us,

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:03.420
particularly those of us
looking after hospitals

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:05.610
where we know we're not
able to offer the type

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.810
of care we would always want to,

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:08.610
to colleagues with mental health,

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:10.170
and that includes mental health trust

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.710
where length of stays over
60 days are continuing

00:34:13.710 --> 00:34:16.210
to not decrease at the
rate that we would like to.

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:21.180
So this is the latest
set of operational data.

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:23.970
What we are aiming to do,
chair, as I mentioned earlier,

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:27.450
is refine this so we can
get better trend analysis.

00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:29.670
You'll see at the end of each of the pack,

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:32.010
there are further areas
we can look to include,

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:35.490
and welcome colleagues' views
on that either at this meeting

00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:38.160
or happy to take comments
outside of this meeting.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:40.230
We're also thinking about how we deep dive

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:41.520
on each of the performance reports.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:44.100
So not at length and we may
do this through subcommittee,

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:46.440
but for example, we know that the work

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:49.830
that Duncan is leading in
maternity has a performance impact

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:51.750
on every region and every system.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.630
Is that an area we would
like to deep dive on?

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:57.960
Sepsis is an area where we're
continuing to focus energy

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:00.720
and actually seeing some
really impressive areas

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:01.553
of performance.

00:35:01.553 --> 00:35:02.760
Is that an area to deep dive on?

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:05.370
So those would be perhaps
the questions are then with,

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:07.470
chair, but happy to take questions

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:09.630
as I know my colleagues would be as well.

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:11.100
- Okay, great. And thank you, Dave.

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.260
And I know we'll have lots of questions.

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:15.390
I might just kick off with a couple,

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:17.460
but if you keep a note, I'll keep a note,

00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:19.436
and then we'll go round.

00:35:19.436 --> 00:35:23.970
I mean I think you didn't
emphasise in the sort of areas

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:28.970
for improvement overall
health and page seven

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:34.700
for me is actually probably
a really important page

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:39.030
which is declining number of
years lived in good health,

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:41.610
really worrying to see that
we know it's happening,

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:43.740
but it's just worrying
to see it that starkly

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.390
and that feels to me like that's a metric

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:46.410
that we should be,

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:49.140
I know that we're not going
to see it change week on week,

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:52.140
but we need to continue to look at that.

00:35:52.140 --> 00:35:53.370
And then, you know,

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:55.860
as you pointed out some of
the other things in there,

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:59.190
cervical cancer screening is
not where we want it to be.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:03.330
Immunizations, MMR is
not where it needs to be

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:05.580
to support population health.

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:08.160
And indeed across all of these metrics,

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:10.443
we know that we have
considerable variation,

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:15.390
and we'll come on and talk
about inequalities later.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:16.740
Exactly as you say,

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:19.620
we should have a big
focus on maternity care.

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:21.720
We've got a lot of really good data there

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:23.430
and I think really helpful that we bring

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:27.300
that a bit more into the
public domain to do so.

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:29.250
So those are the ones from me,

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:30.990
but I know colleagues have got others

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:31.980
that they'd like to raise.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:36.690
So I've got Ravi, I've got
Louise, I've got Duncan,

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:39.210
I've got Andrew, I'll
take those four first.

