00:48:18.217 --> 00:48:19.777
- Just a quick thought
really, David, for the pack,

00:48:19.777 --> 00:48:22.050
I think it's great, but
does risk becoming infinite?

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:24.420
And just a thought from the retail world

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:28.590
as whether we could digitise
it and make it interactive

00:48:28.590 --> 00:48:31.350
and have various lenses on
it so that rather than trying

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:32.880
to print out everything,

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:34.380
if you're wanting to drill into maternity

00:48:34.380 --> 00:48:37.170
or look at various performances,
you just click on the link

00:48:37.170 --> 00:48:39.030
and work it through, put it on an app,

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:42.013
it would be much more
accessible, so just a thought.

00:48:42.013 --> 00:48:43.200
- And a bit more red, amber, green.

00:48:43.200 --> 00:48:44.843
- [Jeremy] Yeah.

00:48:44.843 --> 00:48:45.676
- Meghana.

00:48:45.676 --> 00:48:47.400
- Just wanted to add to
what Sarah-Jane was saying

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:50.160
in terms of urgent emergency care,

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:52.410
clinical leadership obviously is vital.

00:48:52.410 --> 00:48:55.560
So in addition to
collaborating well with care

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:57.750
before coming into excellent emergency

00:48:57.750 --> 00:48:59.670
or emergency departments,

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:01.800
it's the internal processes
that matter as well.

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:06.000
So there are tools like the
iRefer tool for radiology

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:07.830
to reduce unnecessary diagnostics.

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:12.830
I think that is being used and
needs to be used even more.

00:49:13.500 --> 00:49:16.230
Specialist opinion
trying to get specialists

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:19.350
in the first 72 hours
of care within hospitals

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:22.500
is again crucial so that people are seen

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:24.720
and taken to their right destination

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:27.780
for the right care in appropriate time.

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:30.510
And then lastly, again
coming back to tech.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:33.540
So for discharge to stop
the delays and the pharmacy,

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:35.370
take home drugs, et cetera,

00:49:35.370 --> 00:49:38.700
all of those things have to
be implemented consistently

00:49:38.700 --> 00:49:42.480
across all the organisations
to sort of make sure

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:45.300
that the discharge process isn't held up,

00:49:45.300 --> 00:49:49.200
and the focus on all of this
led clinically is something

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:51.270
that we are definitely doing.

00:49:51.270 --> 00:49:54.810
- Okay, so I think let's come back

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:57.990
to probably the next board meeting

00:49:57.990 --> 00:49:59.010
because otherwise it feels

00:49:59.010 --> 00:50:01.560
like the same conversation every year.

00:50:01.560 --> 00:50:04.710
What does our medium term
UEC strategy look like?

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:07.680
I know you've been doing a
lot of work on that. Robert.

00:50:07.680 --> 00:50:10.023
- Just a very quick one in winter prep,

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:12.810
just an update on the vaccination,

00:50:12.810 --> 00:50:16.173
how good the vaccine uptake
has been for flu and COVID.

00:50:17.790 --> 00:50:18.623
- We can bring that,

00:50:18.623 --> 00:50:20.730
I mean for most organisations,
including my own,

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:22.499
that starts on October 1st,

00:50:22.499 --> 00:50:24.930
but we'll make sure we bring the details.

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:27.630
- The paediatric and
the maternity campaigns

00:50:27.630 --> 00:50:30.810
have been launched, but the
general population campaign

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:32.340
starts on the 1st of October.

00:50:32.340 --> 00:50:37.107
So, but yeah, I think it is well piled up,

00:50:37.107 --> 00:50:39.060
and the actions that we committed to each

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:41.430
of those have been ticked off, Robert.

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:43.560
So the ability to be
able to book appointments

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:45.540
and all those kind of
things, we've really done,

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:47.913
taken the learning from
last year on those, so,

00:50:48.924 --> 00:50:50.220
but we'll start to feed the figures

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:52.500
through on a weekly basis
from the beginning of October.

00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:53.970
- Okay, last comment from Rob,

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:55.110
and then we're going to finance.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:57.030
- And just linked onto that vaccination,

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:59.700
there's been a huge response
and energy from the service

00:50:59.700 --> 00:51:02.970
in terms of staff vaccinations
at a couple of sessions

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:06.090
with a 250 attendees on each

00:51:06.090 --> 00:51:08.730
from comms and the clinical community.

00:51:08.730 --> 00:51:10.260
So I think there's a real energy to try

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:11.550
to do even better than-

00:51:11.550 --> 00:51:12.660
- Back to the future Rob-

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:13.560
- Yeah, exactly.

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:16.320
Even better than what's
set out in the UEC strategy

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:18.900
in terms of our, and if we
can get it right for staff,

00:51:18.900 --> 00:51:20.370
that's going to help us I think in terms

00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:22.594
of getting it right for patients as well.

00:51:22.594 --> 00:51:24.481
- Thank you. Elizabeth.

00:51:24.481 --> 00:51:25.314
- Yeah, thank you.

00:51:25.314 --> 00:51:27.330
So moving on to the money then,

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:32.330
financial performance update,
it's at a quite high level,

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:38.190
but currently the paper covers
both revenue and capital.

00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:41.460
It highlights the main
risks as we move forward,

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:42.900
and some of the forward actions,

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:46.530
and I think David and I will
also cover what we intend to do

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:48.510
during the mid-year review.

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:51.330
But first and foremost,

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:52.637
I'd just like to thank
the service actually

00:51:52.637 --> 00:51:55.320
'cause the level of grip that we've seen

00:51:55.320 --> 00:51:57.990
that people have actually continues

00:51:57.990 --> 00:51:59.520
to show up in the numbers.

00:51:59.520 --> 00:52:02.490
So the NHS at month
four is broadly on plan.

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:06.180
We are currently reporting
a 57 million pound overspend

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:07.080
in aggregate,

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:10.860
which is about 0.1% of the
year to date allocation,

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:14.910
which is a significant improvement
on this time last year,

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:18.930
which was close to 500 million.

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:20.070
Key drivers.

00:52:20.070 --> 00:52:22.320
The pressure points that
I talked about last month,

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:23.700
they remain unchanged.

00:52:23.700 --> 00:52:27.060
So we see in missed efficiency
targets and rising workforce

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:32.060
and pay costs, also in the
month four numbers will be some

00:52:32.430 --> 00:52:35.490
of the costs of the five
days of industrial action

00:52:35.490 --> 00:52:37.410
that took place in July.

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:40.677
We know this has had a measurable impact,

00:52:40.677 --> 00:52:43.140
but many providers have
reported back to us

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:46.800
that they've covered these
increased costs in their plans

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:51.800
and we have ensured that
there are two systems

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:55.770
which haven't received their
deficit support funding

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:59.190
as at month four, but that
deficit support funding

00:52:59.190 --> 00:53:01.470
is included in the aggregate numbers.

00:53:01.470 --> 00:53:04.590
You'll also see from the
report, there are six systems

00:53:04.590 --> 00:53:08.040
that account for half
of the overall overspend

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:12.120
and 16 systems and 44% of
providers are currently on plan,

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:14.850
and the full detail is within the pack.

00:53:14.850 --> 00:53:17.100
We continue to forecast that at the end

00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:20.100
of this financial year,
we will deliver the plan

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:22.590
that was signed off earlier in the year

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:25.710
by all of the boards out
there in the systems.

00:53:25.710 --> 00:53:29.040
And we are making sure that
the cash flows appropriately

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:30.210
to keep the business running.

00:53:30.210 --> 00:53:32.400
We have tightened the cash rules,

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:34.050
but we are being very pragmatic

00:53:34.050 --> 00:53:37.410
and working through that very
closely with individuals,

00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:39.210
CFOs, and boards.

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:41.670
Having said this and at
the leadership event,

00:53:41.670 --> 00:53:43.200
we talked about this,

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:46.950
we do know the plans are
back loaded not to the degree

00:53:46.950 --> 00:53:48.510
that it has been in previous years,

00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:52.260
but even so and therefore we
are asking people to be very,

00:53:52.260 --> 00:53:55.170
very focused for the
residual months of this year

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:56.550
and into the second half of the year.

00:53:56.550 --> 00:53:59.340
This midpoint is going
to be absolutely crucial.

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:02.760
So the conversations we are
having around boards needing

00:54:02.760 --> 00:54:07.110
to be clear and not take comfort
from year to date variances

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:09.030
to really look at run rate spend,

00:54:09.030 --> 00:54:11.610
and what's driving their
financial performance.

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:14.250
And we've also signalled at the event

00:54:14.250 --> 00:54:17.100
that we are going to do
a mid-year review process

00:54:17.100 --> 00:54:19.170
that we'll talk about shortly.

00:54:19.170 --> 00:54:22.560
And that is very much
focused on not looking back,

00:54:22.560 --> 00:54:25.440
but making sure that boards
have got credible plans

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:27.300
to deliver not just on finance,

00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:31.263
but across the board for
the remainder of this year.

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:36.090
We have talked quite extensively
to the CFO communities

00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:37.590
that are signalling, you know,

00:54:37.590 --> 00:54:40.140
how challenges looking for
the remainder of the year

00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:43.560
about risks and the opportunity
to mitigate some of those

00:54:43.560 --> 00:54:45.060
through detailed recovery plans

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:47.400
and some of those recovery
plans are in motion.

00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:49.500
And also at month six, we have signalled

00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:52.260
that we're going to do something
called a true-up exercise.

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:56.190
Now this is mainly because
we have got a scenario

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:58.500
where anecdotally we were hearing

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:02.460
that people were stopping some
of their elective recovery

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:04.140
because of finance.

00:55:04.140 --> 00:55:06.930
So we've gone back to have a
look at the reality of that,

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:09.630
and what we are actually
seeing is a very mixed picture.

00:55:09.630 --> 00:55:12.930
So we are seeing that some
providers absolutely seem

00:55:12.930 --> 00:55:16.980
to be constraining in activity
as a consequence of finance

00:55:16.980 --> 00:55:18.060
and there we are going back

00:55:18.060 --> 00:55:21.180
to make sure ICBs have
commissioned the appropriate level

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:24.030
of activity to deliver the standards.

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:26.820
Conversely, we're also
seeing that some providers

00:55:26.820 --> 00:55:29.040
have actually earned income in twelfths,

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:31.020
and they haven't delivered the activity.

00:55:31.020 --> 00:55:33.660
So this month's six exercise
is going to be really important

00:55:33.660 --> 00:55:35.967
because as we think about
what the recovery is

00:55:35.967 --> 00:55:37.140
for the remainder of the year,

00:55:37.140 --> 00:55:39.640
we need to know the issue
we're actually tackling,

00:55:40.503 --> 00:55:41.910
and we need a lot of transparency on this.

00:55:41.910 --> 00:55:43.743
So that's going to be important.

00:55:44.700 --> 00:55:46.410
On capital,

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:50.430
so we've spent about 13% of the
full year budget on capital.

00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:53.730
That's not unusual. So it's 1.28 billion.

00:55:53.730 --> 00:55:57.060
And we are currently
forecasting a 240 million pound

00:55:57.060 --> 00:55:59.310
underspend at the end of this year.

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:01.230
Again, we're going to
do a mid-year review.

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:04.680
It's actually kicked off
where we are asking people

00:56:04.680 --> 00:56:07.800
to really look at their
capital forecasting.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:09.810
The plans don't look as good
as they should have been,

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:12.390
and I think more focus was put on revenue.

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:13.920
And as a consequence of this,

00:56:13.920 --> 00:56:16.140
if people come forward and say
they're going to underspend

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:18.420
on their capital and
they do it early enough,

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:21.450
we're going to look at how
we actually broker this

00:56:21.450 --> 00:56:22.680
across the wider NHS

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:26.460
so that people can carry forward
any capital into next year.

00:56:26.460 --> 00:56:29.130
However, if this happens
in the last quarter

00:56:29.130 --> 00:56:32.460
or towards back end of the
year in an unplanned way,

00:56:32.460 --> 00:56:34.950
then that is going to be a
bit of a travesty to be honest

00:56:34.950 --> 00:56:35.783
'cause we are not,

00:56:35.783 --> 00:56:37.590
it's going to be too late
for us to do anything.

00:56:37.590 --> 00:56:40.500
And capital is a hugely scarce resource.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:43.170
So no surprises we're asking for,

00:56:43.170 --> 00:56:47.310
and I keep asking wherever I go,

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:49.500
please just keep talking
to myself and the team

00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:50.523
around capital.

00:56:51.630 --> 00:56:53.760
And then on to just
quickly emerging risks,

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:58.470
I suppose it's the unfunded
redundancy across ICBs

00:56:58.470 --> 00:57:01.170
and obviously national bodies,

00:57:01.170 --> 00:57:04.980
it's cost pressures in especially
specialised commissioning,

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:06.660
particularly high cost devices.

00:57:06.660 --> 00:57:09.810
And I think I mentioned this
in a previous board meeting,

00:57:09.810 --> 00:57:13.360
that we have got a scenario
where there are cost effective

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:19.640
high cost devices that providers
and clinicians could use,

00:57:20.910 --> 00:57:23.940
sorry, could use a more
cost effective device,

00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:25.500
but they are still choosing even though

00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:27.000
that there's not a clinical reason

00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:28.260
to choose the higher cost one.

00:57:28.260 --> 00:57:30.570
So we are looking at how
we reimburse on that,

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:33.780
even partway through the year,
we may change our approach.

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:36.750
And then the other material
risk will be the further risk

00:57:36.750 --> 00:57:38.460
of industrial action.

00:57:38.460 --> 00:57:42.780
Within the paper as well,
there is an update on VPAG,

00:57:42.780 --> 00:57:45.120
an industrial engagement, chair,

00:57:45.120 --> 00:57:48.930
where VPAG is Voluntary Scheme
for Branded Medicine Pricing,

00:57:48.930 --> 00:57:49.950
Access and Growth.

00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:53.430
It's a bit of a mouthful, which
is why we shortened to VPAG.

00:57:53.430 --> 00:57:56.577
And really it's just to
conclude, I suppose, you know,

00:57:56.577 --> 00:58:01.577
the NHS has engaged with
pharma companies to look at,

00:58:04.080 --> 00:58:08.610
and proactively engage about
how we might look at working

00:58:08.610 --> 00:58:12.720
with the industry in
a more responsive way.

00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:14.850
All of the proposals have
been put forward to date.

00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:17.070
The pharma companies
have responded negatively

00:58:17.070 --> 00:58:21.510
to including withdrawal of
new drugs from the UK market.

00:58:21.510 --> 00:58:24.660
And as a consequence, we are
where we are at the moment,

00:58:24.660 --> 00:58:28.290
we are, we remain with
our existing arrangements,

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:32.010
and I'm sure we will wait
to see what happens next.

00:58:32.010 --> 00:58:35.073
- Okay, thank you.
Questions for Elizabeth.

00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:38.933
Andrew.

00:58:38.933 --> 00:58:42.360
- Can I just raise a point
around the deficit support,

00:58:42.360 --> 00:58:45.453
the 2.2 billion that's gone in,

00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:50.220
is that tapered, Elizabeth,
for over a number of years?

00:58:50.220 --> 00:58:53.130
Or is it a cliff edge that providers drop

00:58:53.130 --> 00:58:56.460
or systems drop at 31st of March?

00:58:56.460 --> 00:59:00.870
- Yeah, so it's annual and
in the 10-year health plan,

00:59:00.870 --> 00:59:02.940
we signalled that we would move away

00:59:02.940 --> 00:59:06.120
from the deficit support funding approach

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:07.620
through the work that we've been doing

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:12.120
with extensive engagement,
actually over 600 people to date

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:14.400
around some of this, around
the allocation formula.

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:16.050
We are looking to actually put some

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:18.900
of our deficit support
funding into core allocations

00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:20.770
to move people closer to target

00:59:22.094 --> 00:59:24.540
and the withdrawal of deficit support,

00:59:24.540 --> 00:59:26.550
how it's actually described
in the 10-year health plan,

00:59:26.550 --> 00:59:27.420
which we are working through,

00:59:27.420 --> 00:59:31.604
is we will start to
withdraw it from '26, '27.

00:59:31.604 --> 00:59:34.860
- [Andrew] And if you are
overspent, let's take an example,

00:59:34.860 --> 00:59:39.860
blanks, they've got 164 mil of
support, they're over by 20.

00:59:40.470 --> 00:59:42.780
So what happens at the
year end if they've had

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:44.280
that money and they're still over?

00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:45.540
- So-

00:59:45.540 --> 00:59:47.823
- [Andrew] Does the overcommitment,

00:59:47.823 --> 00:59:50.520
does that get locked
off the following year

00:59:50.520 --> 00:59:52.680
or is it just written off?

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:54.420
- [Elizabeth] The
overcommitment, sorry, sorry.

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:59.420
- [Andrew] So if the outturn
is more than 164 deficit-

00:59:59.640 --> 01:00:00.741
- [Elizabeth] Yeah.

01:00:00.741 --> 01:00:02.040
- [Andrew] For that particular system,

01:00:02.040 --> 01:00:06.000
what happens to that lump of
cash that they've exceeded?

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:09.450
- Yeah, so if in for example,

01:00:09.450 --> 01:00:14.392
a system actually does
not deliver its plan-

01:00:14.392 --> 01:00:15.225
- [Andrew] Yeah.

01:00:15.225 --> 01:00:19.830
- The deficit support funding is withheld.

01:00:19.830 --> 01:00:22.140
At the end of the year,
that could be reallocated.

01:00:22.140 --> 01:00:25.380
We'll make sure that there's
cash to pay staff and bills.

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:29.250
However, if a system goes off
plan at the end of the year,

01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:31.200
that is carried forward effectively

01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:34.080
and it goes into the
historic debt profiling

01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:36.626
so you don't get it written off.

01:00:36.626 --> 01:00:37.459
- [Andrew] Okay.

01:00:37.459 --> 01:00:38.880
- I think we need to be really clear

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:43.260
that the deficit support
funding will flow this year

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:45.162
if people are on plan.

01:00:45.162 --> 01:00:46.680
- [Andrew] Okay, thank you.

01:00:46.680 --> 01:00:48.453
- I'll just follow up on that one

01:00:48.453 --> 01:00:50.253
and then take some wider questions.

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:53.820
First off is, in that annex one,

01:00:53.820 --> 01:00:56.340
could we have a 1A and a 1B?

01:00:56.340 --> 01:01:01.340
So 1A shown as it is, but 1B
ordering by deficit support

01:01:02.850 --> 01:01:05.490
so we can see the sort of true costs.

01:01:05.490 --> 01:01:09.600
The second is when we start
getting systems coming back

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:14.600
with their medium term plans,
will we be able to see,

01:01:16.710 --> 01:01:20.550
is this system overspent
because they're spending money

01:01:20.550 --> 01:01:25.413
on either care which
doesn't impact health?

01:01:26.610 --> 01:01:31.140
So they're not allocating
money in the most impactful way

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:34.320
or because they are
spending their unit cost,

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:37.620
either provider costs are
higher than they should be,

01:01:37.620 --> 01:01:41.130
i.e., they're paying for
inefficient services.

01:01:41.130 --> 01:01:43.980
Will we be able to see
where they are on that,

01:01:43.980 --> 01:01:46.620
i.e., are they, is it
allocative efficiency

01:01:46.620 --> 01:01:47.910
or is it technical efficiency?

01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:49.200
That's their challenge.

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:50.033
- Yeah. Okay.

01:01:51.150 --> 01:01:53.130
- Do you think we will be
able to see that when they-

01:01:53.130 --> 01:01:55.050
- I think we'll have,

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:57.420
I'm thinking of the
timing of some exercises.

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:00.870
So the bit about the
efficient delivery of care,

01:02:00.870 --> 01:02:03.270
you would usually see through
reference cost indices,

01:02:03.270 --> 01:02:05.520
but that happens at a different timeframe.

01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:07.080
We've got lots of points of data.

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:10.290
They wouldn't all coincide
with a medium term plan.

01:02:10.290 --> 01:02:12.570
So I think we would look
to triangulate that.

01:02:12.570 --> 01:02:14.040
What we will have though, Penny,

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:18.180
is we'll have the very
detailed productivity plaques

01:02:18.180 --> 01:02:21.150
that we can overlay against
the medium term plan to see

01:02:21.150 --> 01:02:25.020
what the scale of the
opportunity is at that point

01:02:25.020 --> 01:02:26.280
and whether or not they're factoring

01:02:26.280 --> 01:02:28.200
that into the plan to address it.

01:02:28.200 --> 01:02:29.963
- Okay, thank you.

01:02:29.963 --> 01:02:33.183
So I've got Louise, I've
got Robert, I've got David.

01:02:34.230 --> 01:02:35.063
- Thanks, Elizabeth.

01:02:35.063 --> 01:02:40.063
So how can we make sure that
patient care isn't impacted

01:02:40.410 --> 01:02:43.890
if trade offs are being made by systems

01:02:43.890 --> 01:02:45.900
in order to reach their
efficiency targets?

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:48.660
You touched on it a
bit with the electives.

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:50.430
- [Penny] Yep, let me
take all the questions

01:02:50.430 --> 01:02:51.630
and come back to that.

01:02:51.630 --> 01:02:52.770
Robert.

01:02:52.770 --> 01:02:57.750
- A comment on the question
on the drug pricing challenge,

01:02:57.750 --> 01:03:00.300
I think this is a very,
very menacing threat.

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:01.950
It's had a lot of press coverage

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:03.840
so you refer to withdrawing drugs,

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:07.260
but AstraZeneca pulling back
on its investment in Cambridge

01:03:07.260 --> 01:03:10.110
and Merck pulling out
completely of its building

01:03:10.110 --> 01:03:11.343
in King's Cross.

01:03:12.720 --> 01:03:15.300
One of the government's
top priorities is growth.

01:03:15.300 --> 01:03:17.700
So I don't feel that the burden

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:20.010
should appropriately
fall entirely on the NHS.

01:03:20.010 --> 01:03:22.500
I mean this is a national issue,

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:25.050
but the NHS should lobby
for its importance in terms

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:27.810
of us being at the
forefront of innovation.

01:03:27.810 --> 01:03:30.840
So my question I suppose is
the steps that you're taking,

01:03:30.840 --> 01:03:33.933
which are absolutely
terrific and appropriate,

01:03:35.100 --> 01:03:37.830
how reassuring can you be
that this is registering

01:03:37.830 --> 01:03:39.390
and it's going to turn things around?

01:03:39.390 --> 01:03:41.970
I forgot to add that the
problem is intensified of course

01:03:41.970 --> 01:03:43.110
by the Trump administration,

01:03:43.110 --> 01:03:45.420
which is bearing down
on drug costs in the US

01:03:45.420 --> 01:03:48.240
which have been bankrolling
pharma until now.

01:03:48.240 --> 01:03:49.650
And so it's not surprising,

01:03:49.650 --> 01:03:52.230
but it's coming home to roost here.

01:03:52.230 --> 01:03:53.520
- [Penny] David.

01:03:53.520 --> 01:03:56.250
- Just a small technical
point to reinforce your point

01:03:56.250 --> 01:03:58.080
about making the tables more useful,

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:00.840
can we put a column in with these numbers

01:04:00.840 --> 01:04:02.970
as a percentage of turnover?

01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:04.530
'Cause it's difficult to judge

01:04:04.530 --> 01:04:06.780
how significant these
numbers are without that.

01:04:06.780 --> 01:04:08.430
- Yeah.

01:04:08.430 --> 01:04:12.480
- Okay, so quality versus cost,

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:15.570
and then the pharma industry.

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:16.650
- Yeah, so absolutely.

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:21.650
So Louise on the quality,
you know, and cost dynamic.

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:24.030
So all of the work that we are doing,

01:04:24.030 --> 01:04:26.760
we are consistently asking those questions

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:29.253
and asking for quality impact assessments.

01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:33.390
We don't in finance,
definitely not in this team,

01:04:33.390 --> 01:04:35.910
work in isolation of
our clinical colleagues.

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:38.100
So that is a first and foremost.

01:04:38.100 --> 01:04:43.100
And also as we are making the
CIPs or productivity savings,

01:04:43.500 --> 01:04:46.980
we triangulate them with a
number of quality indicators.

01:04:46.980 --> 01:04:48.900
So you know, if we are looking at some

01:04:48.900 --> 01:04:51.780
of our policy changes, we are
looking at readmissions rates,

01:04:51.780 --> 01:04:52.920
we are looking at harm,

01:04:52.920 --> 01:04:55.320
we are looking at some
of those mechanisms.

01:04:55.320 --> 01:04:59.310
And in some of the efficiency
savings we'll also have a,

01:04:59.310 --> 01:05:02.040
it's called waste, which I
know that waste and harm,

01:05:02.040 --> 01:05:03.750
I think people don't
like those terms in terms

01:05:03.750 --> 01:05:05.910
of the buckets, but we
are constantly driving

01:05:05.910 --> 01:05:08.453
and making sure that the
conversations that we're having

01:05:08.453 --> 01:05:11.280
and are both with the finance community,

01:05:11.280 --> 01:05:14.430
but also our clinical colleagues.

01:05:14.430 --> 01:05:17.580
So I feel there's a lot
of work that goes on.

01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:20.760
I can definitely bring it out
in the report going forward.

01:05:20.760 --> 01:05:22.980
Touching on something
that Jeremy described,

01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:26.040
we've got so much information maybe again,

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:27.870
it's about enabling a drill down,

01:05:27.870 --> 01:05:30.750
'cause whichever way I present it,

01:05:30.750 --> 01:05:33.400
we could always present it a different way

01:05:33.400 --> 01:05:35.070
and people will be looking
at different things

01:05:35.070 --> 01:05:39.870
and then just touching on the
report that David's described,

01:05:39.870 --> 01:05:42.120
we're trying to bring it
to make it more holistic

01:05:42.120 --> 01:05:43.740
so that you can look across a range

01:05:43.740 --> 01:05:46.800
of indicators to see that
triangulation yourselves.

01:05:46.800 --> 01:05:50.010
- Okay, one thing it would
be great for us to get to,

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:51.270
and I recognise it's not easy,

01:05:51.270 --> 01:05:53.940
but it's a bit linked
to what I said before,

01:05:53.940 --> 01:05:57.690
is we should be looking
to say to what degree

01:05:57.690 --> 01:06:00.900
are we allocating money
to maximise the health

01:06:00.900 --> 01:06:01.830
of the population,

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:05.580
which is our ultimate remit is to do that.

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:08.130
So we ought to be able to say,

01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:12.960
if we were really spending money in order

01:06:12.960 --> 01:06:14.490
to improve health outcomes,

01:06:14.490 --> 01:06:16.590
where do we think we would
be spending the money?

01:06:16.590 --> 01:06:18.645
What would be the sorts of
services we'd be spending

01:06:18.645 --> 01:06:23.490
the money on and how close are
we to that in each ICB sort

01:06:23.490 --> 01:06:25.320
of, you know, we don't have that yet,

01:06:25.320 --> 01:06:27.240
but we ought to be able to do that.

01:06:27.240 --> 01:06:30.240
And then the second bit, which
I know you are working on,

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:32.790
is to say how do we define

01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:34.320
from the national service frameworks,

01:06:34.320 --> 01:06:37.230
the modern service frameworks,
the care pathway design,

01:06:37.230 --> 01:06:40.140
what does really high
quality care look like?

01:06:40.140 --> 01:06:43.080
And crucially, what should that cost?

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:45.810
'Cause we think that it
probably should cost less

01:06:45.810 --> 01:06:47.130
than what we're spending now,

01:06:47.130 --> 01:06:49.890
because we have big productivity
opportunities in there.

01:06:49.890 --> 01:06:51.300
But if we could define that,

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:54.600
I think more clearly and demonstrate that,

01:06:54.600 --> 01:06:57.210
that should give assurance that driving

01:06:57.210 --> 01:07:00.840
on improved productivity
and getting the money

01:07:00.840 --> 01:07:03.900
to work better is actually
focusing on improving quality,

01:07:03.900 --> 01:07:05.070
not distracting from it.

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:07.320
- [Elizabeth] Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:09.330
- So you want to talk about
the pharma industry piece?

01:07:09.330 --> 01:07:12.033
- Yeah, so I think in the
report we highlight the way

01:07:12.033 --> 01:07:15.420
that NHS England's actually
continuing to engage

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:19.080
with the industry and trying
to be incredibly proactive.

01:07:19.080 --> 01:07:22.770
But as you've described, I
think the scale of the risk,

01:07:22.770 --> 01:07:25.530
it's bigger than just for the NHS.

01:07:25.530 --> 01:07:29.550
I think we, I get a sense we
are absolutely being heard

01:07:29.550 --> 01:07:33.450
in terms of our conversations
both through with colleagues

01:07:33.450 --> 01:07:36.050
at the Department of Health
and through to Treasury.

01:07:38.800 --> 01:07:41.580
I'm not sure there's much
more I can say at this stage

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:45.900
if I'm honest, but the
conversations are live,

01:07:45.900 --> 01:07:50.250
and I guess what we should do
as NHS England at the moment

01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:53.280
is continue to build those relationships,

01:07:53.280 --> 01:07:56.040
invest in the things that only we can do.

01:07:56.040 --> 01:08:00.690
And I think that some
of the wider decisions

01:08:00.690 --> 01:08:04.350
around macro investment have to happen,

01:08:04.350 --> 01:08:07.053
those decisions have to happen elsewhere.

01:08:08.880 --> 01:08:10.560
- Okay, thank you. Right.

01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:12.990
So we've gone through performance,

01:08:12.990 --> 01:08:15.660
we're now saying what are
we going to do about it.

01:08:15.660 --> 01:08:18.031
Jim, 10-year plan.

01:08:18.031 --> 01:08:18.864
- Yeah.

01:08:18.864 --> 01:08:20.010
- I think to pick up
Paul's point from earlier,

01:08:20.010 --> 01:08:23.550
the risks of us not
reforming and adapting.

01:08:23.550 --> 01:08:24.510
- Absolutely.

01:08:24.510 --> 01:08:27.690
So as we discussed earlier
on the 10-year plan

01:08:27.690 --> 01:08:29.760
was published, landed very well,

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:32.130
lots of enthusiasm in the service.

01:08:32.130 --> 01:08:36.480
We have tried very hard to
resist issue in a big dock,

01:08:36.480 --> 01:08:39.630
big delivery plan with
lots of instructions in.

01:08:39.630 --> 01:08:42.210
We've worked with colleagues
in the service around a bunch

01:08:42.210 --> 01:08:44.940
of work streams that are
all about bringing the plan

01:08:44.940 --> 01:08:46.200
to life.

01:08:46.200 --> 01:08:50.640
We're now in a process of
refining those mainly round impact

01:08:50.640 --> 01:08:54.330
to make sure we know that
these things have a purpose

01:08:54.330 --> 01:08:56.880
in the end and there's
a quantifiable purpose.

01:08:56.880 --> 01:09:00.330
I would expect that to be
complete in the next week or so.

01:09:00.330 --> 01:09:02.820
And then we're trying to dovetail this

01:09:02.820 --> 01:09:06.630
into the medium term plan process,

01:09:06.630 --> 01:09:10.560
which we're still aiming to
get out into the service end

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:12.303
of September, early October.

01:09:13.296 --> 01:09:16.143
There's an awful lot to be
done to be able to do that.

01:09:17.516 --> 01:09:19.380
A key part of all that,
as you said, Penny,

01:09:19.380 --> 01:09:21.210
is to try to get the balance right

01:09:21.210 --> 01:09:24.750
between short term
imperatives and long term,

01:09:24.750 --> 01:09:27.870
but also the extent of which
these long-term changes

01:09:27.870 --> 01:09:31.170
actually address our short-term problems.

01:09:31.170 --> 01:09:34.680
And we're trying very hard
with colleagues to just keep

01:09:34.680 --> 01:09:39.237
that balance right and get
the prioritisation appropriate

01:09:39.237 --> 01:09:41.310
and the sequencing appropriate in terms

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:44.040
of what happens next year
and in subsequent years.

01:09:44.040 --> 01:09:45.900
So I think we're in very good shape.

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:47.756
I think there is a, if we're honest,

01:09:47.756 --> 01:09:50.460
there is a tension with this
thing about not giving lots

01:09:50.460 --> 01:09:54.180
of instruction where I think
that's generally welcomed.

01:09:54.180 --> 01:09:56.790
But every now and again,
you can have a conversation

01:09:56.790 --> 01:09:58.590
that says I really don't
want lots of instruction,

01:09:58.590 --> 01:10:00.140
but can you tell us what to do?

01:10:01.530 --> 01:10:03.340
And we just have to live with that.

01:10:03.340 --> 01:10:05.220
We're in transition phases.

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:07.410
It's a difficult process that we're in.

01:10:07.410 --> 01:10:11.250
But I think really
fantastic progress so far.

01:10:11.250 --> 01:10:12.330
- Okay, good.

01:10:12.330 --> 01:10:13.770
And look forward to seeing more

01:10:13.770 --> 01:10:17.490
of those subsequent pieces evolving.

01:10:17.490 --> 01:10:18.330
Okay, great.

01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:20.520
So let's move on to a conversation

01:10:20.520 --> 01:10:22.590
about healthcare inequalities

01:10:22.590 --> 01:10:25.290
or rather healthcare inequalities
improvement programme.

01:10:26.340 --> 01:10:29.130
Dianne, Habib, over to you.

01:10:29.130 --> 01:10:31.350
Assume the paper has been read,

01:10:31.350 --> 01:10:36.350
but if you want to just say a
couple of points in summary,

01:10:37.133 --> 01:10:39.900
it'd be good and then we'll
open it up for questions.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:41.670
- Thank you very much.

01:10:41.670 --> 01:10:46.670
I took over from Professor
Bola Owolabi a month ago

01:10:47.130 --> 01:10:52.130
and have been working with the
programme to move it forward

01:10:52.380 --> 01:10:55.413
for the future, for the
work set for the future.

01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:59.940
As Penny has said, I take
the paper as being read

01:10:59.940 --> 01:11:03.930
and I thought I would just
highlight two specific examples

01:11:03.930 --> 01:11:08.130
of impact that we've
delivered over the last year.

01:11:08.130 --> 01:11:11.070
So if you take the elective reform

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:14.490
and it's been very helpful
hearing about the progress

01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:18.030
with the performance as David outlined,

01:11:18.030 --> 01:11:23.030
he talked about electives in
terms of the granular data

01:11:23.130 --> 01:11:24.450
that has been published.

01:11:24.450 --> 01:11:26.280
And I think in the discussion,

01:11:26.280 --> 01:11:30.780
we talked about the
average hiding the detail

01:11:30.780 --> 01:11:32.700
between what is really going on,

01:11:32.700 --> 01:11:35.190
who is doing well, and
who is not doing well.

01:11:35.190 --> 01:11:37.248
And one thing we've pushed with the

01:11:37.248 --> 01:11:38.731
elective reform programme,

01:11:38.731 --> 01:11:41.760
we've worked very
collaboratively with them

01:11:41.760 --> 01:11:46.260
and we outlined a healthcare
inequalities improvement ask.

01:11:46.260 --> 01:11:50.880
And you would see that in
July, the data was published,

01:11:50.880 --> 01:11:55.880
granular data was published
disaggregated by age, by sex,

01:11:56.850 --> 01:12:01.350
by deprivation, and by ethnicity.

01:12:01.350 --> 01:12:04.320
And that highlighted
where the challenges were,

01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:06.420
which groups had challenges,

01:12:06.420 --> 01:12:08.640
and what differences could be made

01:12:08.640 --> 01:12:10.650
by addressing those challenges.

01:12:10.650 --> 01:12:12.720
So we see for example

01:12:12.720 --> 01:12:17.010
that people in deprived populations
are waiting much longer.

01:12:17.010 --> 01:12:20.940
We see for example, women
are waiting much longer.

01:12:20.940 --> 01:12:22.260
We see for example

01:12:22.260 --> 01:12:25.890
that people in working ages
are waiting much longer.

01:12:25.890 --> 01:12:29.730
This had previously not
happened and it's, you know,

01:12:29.730 --> 01:12:32.820
with the work that we've done,
that has been highlighted,

01:12:32.820 --> 01:12:34.590
and we are now working with systems

01:12:34.590 --> 01:12:37.140
to say we've highlighted the problem.

01:12:37.140 --> 01:12:39.210
What are you doing about it?

01:12:39.210 --> 01:12:41.940
These are some of the
things you can do about it.

01:12:41.940 --> 01:12:45.990
The second point I wanted to
highlight as a success has been

01:12:45.990 --> 01:12:48.210
that with executive support,

01:12:48.210 --> 01:12:51.630
we actually started supporting systems to

01:12:51.630 --> 01:12:55.740
deliver a quality improvement
programme with sickle cell.

01:12:55.740 --> 01:12:59.490
We had significant challenges with UEC.

01:12:59.490 --> 01:13:02.160
The main reason a
patient with sickle cell,

01:13:02.160 --> 01:13:03.557
we'll go to the emergency room

01:13:03.557 --> 01:13:06.330
is for an uncomplicated crisis.

01:13:06.330 --> 01:13:10.050
They utilise the emergency
room high intensity users.

01:13:10.050 --> 01:13:13.290
What we've done is we have over the year

01:13:13.290 --> 01:13:18.210
delivered seven emergency
department bypass units.

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:21.120
I visited Lewisham last week.

01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:25.170
They have for the very first
time since NICE published

01:13:25.170 --> 01:13:30.170
its guidance in 2012 about
achieving a 30-minute target

01:13:31.140 --> 01:13:35.130
when patients come to emergency
have for the very first time

01:13:35.130 --> 01:13:37.320
delivered pain relief,

01:13:37.320 --> 01:13:42.320
97% of the time hasn't
previously happened,

01:13:42.420 --> 01:13:45.423
spoke to patients, engaged with patients,

01:13:46.560 --> 01:13:51.000
has made a significant
improvement in their experience,

01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:53.880
spending less time in hospital,

01:13:53.880 --> 01:13:56.490
has decreased admission time,

01:13:56.490 --> 01:13:58.830
has improved patient experience.

01:13:58.830 --> 01:14:03.510
Patients are going home much
earlier, improved productivity.

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:06.150
They were not utilising clinicians,

01:14:06.150 --> 01:14:08.430
they have proposed a different model

01:14:08.430 --> 01:14:11.700
where they've used advanced
clinical practitioners.

01:14:11.700 --> 01:14:15.930
So it hasn't been so much of
a challenge to get people in.

01:14:15.930 --> 01:14:20.640
Moving forward, we have proposed
in our report you would see

01:14:20.640 --> 01:14:24.540
to pivot our work programme
to enable delivery

01:14:24.540 --> 01:14:26.790
of the 10-year plan.

01:14:26.790 --> 01:14:30.750
We are also rephrasing our narrative

01:14:30.750 --> 01:14:33.270
so that it takes on board the challenges

01:14:33.270 --> 01:14:37.890
the system currently faces and
the priorities that are going

01:14:37.890 --> 01:14:40.440
to be delivered by the system.

01:14:40.440 --> 01:14:44.100
We will be focusing on
working with various teams,

01:14:44.100 --> 01:14:48.120
the finance team to
address resource allocation

01:14:48.120 --> 01:14:51.120
so that it is balanced and as Penny said,

01:14:51.120 --> 01:14:56.070
it addresses the issue of
how will we allocate finances

01:14:56.070 --> 01:14:58.590
if we want to improve outcomes and care

01:14:58.590 --> 01:15:00.630
and reduce healthcare inequalities.

01:15:00.630 --> 01:15:03.030
We'll be doing that work
with the finance team

01:15:03.030 --> 01:15:05.100
and we've prioritised that.

01:15:05.100 --> 01:15:08.250
We'll be working with the
system development team

01:15:08.250 --> 01:15:11.220
to hardwire addressing inequalities

01:15:11.220 --> 01:15:13.260
through all the work we are doing.

01:15:13.260 --> 01:15:16.560
If we are not going to be
asking the system explicitly

01:15:16.560 --> 01:15:18.960
every year in planning
guidance that this is

01:15:18.960 --> 01:15:20.820
what you should be delivering,

01:15:20.820 --> 01:15:24.780
then it's got to be in the
system so that they know

01:15:24.780 --> 01:15:26.700
that this is what we should be doing

01:15:26.700 --> 01:15:30.390
and this is what good looks
like if we are addressing

01:15:30.390 --> 01:15:32.490
what health inequalities are.

01:15:32.490 --> 01:15:35.580
So we'll be working with
the system development team

01:15:35.580 --> 01:15:37.500
to ensure that happens,

01:15:37.500 --> 01:15:40.110
whether it's about
strategic commissioning,

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:42.660
whether it's about
developing integrated health

01:15:42.660 --> 01:15:46.680
organisations or whether it's
a foundation trust contract.

01:15:46.680 --> 01:15:48.360
So we'll be doing that.

01:15:48.360 --> 01:15:52.080
We'll also be working with, and
David doesn't know this yet,

01:15:52.080 --> 01:15:54.630
but we'll be working with you to-

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:57.240
- I'm a bit concerned, we want
to have time for questions.

01:15:57.240 --> 01:15:58.449
- [Dianne] Sorry. Okay.

01:15:58.449 --> 01:16:00.180
- So you've set up here
your six priorities.

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:02.607
So I think we've got that, we understand,

01:16:02.607 --> 01:16:05.340
and we'll come on and ask
some questions about that.

01:16:05.340 --> 01:16:07.590
- Underlying all this will have to be data

01:16:07.590 --> 01:16:09.810
and so I'll be working with Ming on that,

01:16:09.810 --> 01:16:12.750
to ensure the granularity
of the data feeds

01:16:12.750 --> 01:16:14.070
through all this.

01:16:14.070 --> 01:16:15.168
- [Penny] Okay.

01:16:15.168 --> 01:16:16.200
- I'll pass on to Habib.

01:16:16.200 --> 01:16:17.250
- I'll be very quick.

01:16:18.630 --> 01:16:20.820
The observatory continues
to be a delivery partner

01:16:20.820 --> 01:16:22.920
for NHS England and for
the Department of Health

01:16:22.920 --> 01:16:23.850
and Social Care.

01:16:23.850 --> 01:16:26.940
Our role is not just to highlight
the scale of the challenge

01:16:26.940 --> 01:16:28.230
but to provide solutions.

01:16:28.230 --> 01:16:31.290
And on that basis, I'll
highlight three quick examples.

01:16:31.290 --> 01:16:33.570
Firstly, bringing together
evidence and insight,

01:16:33.570 --> 01:16:36.450
our partnership with the
National Institute for Health

01:16:36.450 --> 01:16:39.240
and Care Research to build in equity

01:16:39.240 --> 01:16:44.010
and inclusion within the criteria
for commissioning research

01:16:44.010 --> 01:16:46.323
is ongoing and progressing.

01:16:47.190 --> 01:16:50.580
We are bringing together secondly,

01:16:50.580 --> 01:16:51.990
in terms of driving policy change,

01:16:51.990 --> 01:16:53.070
we are bringing together all

01:16:53.070 --> 01:16:57.480
of the healthcare
regulatory bodies to work

01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:00.150
from the same page when it
comes to equity and inclusion

01:17:00.150 --> 01:17:04.200
in terms of accountability,
which is a key lever in terms

01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:06.990
of shifting the dial on inequality.

01:17:06.990 --> 01:17:09.360
We have a groundbreaking
partnership with NICE,

01:17:09.360 --> 01:17:12.180
the National Institute for
Health and Care Excellence

01:17:12.180 --> 01:17:14.520
to de-bias current clinical guidelines

01:17:14.520 --> 01:17:16.350
across a range of areas.

01:17:16.350 --> 01:17:20.040
And also working with them to
build processes within NICE

01:17:20.040 --> 01:17:21.960
so that the development
of any new guidelines

01:17:21.960 --> 01:17:24.870
do not have those biases
built in from the outset.

01:17:24.870 --> 01:17:27.930
And thirdly, in terms of
implementation support,

01:17:27.930 --> 01:17:30.180
we're working with eight ICSs

01:17:30.180 --> 01:17:34.410
to look at closing down
the maternity mortality gap

01:17:34.410 --> 01:17:38.130
by ethnicity and looking at
neonatal testing as well.

01:17:38.130 --> 01:17:42.090
And with, or in collaboration
with Duncan and the team,

01:17:42.090 --> 01:17:45.690
we rolled out mannequins
and birthing stimulators

01:17:45.690 --> 01:17:48.270
in different skin tones for
the first time in the history

01:17:48.270 --> 01:17:52.410
of the NHS across every
maternity unit in the country.

01:17:52.410 --> 01:17:55.530
And working on mental health,

01:17:55.530 --> 01:17:58.710
Gypsy, Roma Traveller
mental health as well.

01:17:58.710 --> 01:18:00.720
Looking ahead very quickly,

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:02.880
we're engaging with a 10-year plan team

01:18:02.880 --> 01:18:05.610
and the neighbourhood health services team

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:07.800
to look at building levels of trust

01:18:07.800 --> 01:18:12.783
and confidence in healthcare
services more generally.

01:18:13.620 --> 01:18:16.620
And of course finally
if we're in the business

01:18:16.620 --> 01:18:18.840
of tackling health and
inequalities, which we are,

01:18:18.840 --> 01:18:20.670
then we need a workforce
as fully supported

01:18:20.670 --> 01:18:22.950
and engaged at all levels.

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:25.740
So we're focusing on looking

01:18:25.740 --> 01:18:30.740
at the independent ethnicity
pay and progression gap

01:18:30.780 --> 01:18:32.820
across the NHS workforce,

01:18:32.820 --> 01:18:35.550
and secondly looking at
closing down the gap in terms

01:18:35.550 --> 01:18:38.040
of bullying and harassment by ethnicity,

01:18:38.040 --> 01:18:40.834
but also decreasing the
overall levels of bullying

01:18:40.834 --> 01:18:43.684
and harassment across the
whole of the workforce as well.

01:18:44.545 --> 01:18:45.960
- Great, thank you.

01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:50.960
Okay, so questions, I've
got Duncan, Louise, and Sam.

01:18:52.470 --> 01:18:54.750
- Yeah, thanks. I guess
just a couple of points.

01:18:54.750 --> 01:18:57.180
One, the first being
about are we doing enough

01:18:57.180 --> 01:18:59.400
to integrate into the broader strategies

01:18:59.400 --> 01:19:00.837
that we've got under
development at the moment?

01:19:00.837 --> 01:19:03.476
And I was, we reflected the
other day, Dianne, you know,

01:19:03.476 --> 01:19:04.309
obviously there's a piece of work,

01:19:04.309 --> 01:19:07.140
we're looking at the LGBT
health evidence at the moment,

01:19:07.140 --> 01:19:08.430
and we make sure that permeates

01:19:08.430 --> 01:19:10.260
through all the other strategies

01:19:10.260 --> 01:19:11.610
so it doesn't sit in isolation.

01:19:11.610 --> 01:19:15.300
So just any reflections on what
we do enough in that space.

01:19:15.300 --> 01:19:17.970
And then the race and health observatories

01:19:17.970 --> 01:19:22.140
got a really respected
brand associated with it.

01:19:22.140 --> 01:19:24.300
It's doing a phenomenal amount of work

01:19:24.300 --> 01:19:26.280
and I'm just wondering
whether we are doing enough

01:19:26.280 --> 01:19:30.170
to leverage the impact
across the NHS with all

01:19:30.170 --> 01:19:32.020
of the parts of that.

01:19:32.020 --> 01:19:34.821
And I just welcome some
reflections on that.

01:19:34.821 --> 01:19:37.324
- Okay, I'm going to take all
the questions first. Louise.

01:19:37.324 --> 01:19:38.157
Yeah.

01:19:38.157 --> 01:19:39.390
- So yeah, thank you, Habib and Dianne,

01:19:39.390 --> 01:19:42.840
for your fantastic work and
that your reports were great.

01:19:42.840 --> 01:19:44.043
So two questions.

01:19:44.935 --> 01:19:47.790
The first is, in your view,
how should funding flows change

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:50.787
to reduce inequalities and health?

01:19:50.787 --> 01:19:52.920
And the second is about racism

01:19:52.920 --> 01:19:57.570
of which we've seen some
manifestation in public recently.

01:19:57.570 --> 01:20:01.530
What do you think the
NHS's role is in helping

01:20:01.530 --> 01:20:02.400
to reduce racism

01:20:02.400 --> 01:20:05.313
because we know it has a
significant impact on health?

01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:09.423
- [Penny] Okay. Sam?

01:20:10.620 --> 01:20:13.680
- Yeah, Penny rightly pointed
out in the performance report

01:20:13.680 --> 01:20:16.440
page seven, which is really worrying,

01:20:16.440 --> 01:20:18.813
and a drop in healthy life expectancy.

01:20:20.160 --> 01:20:23.220
And in my community it's even
worse, in some ethnic groups,

01:20:23.220 --> 01:20:27.390
women are living shorter
lives and more unhealthy lives

01:20:27.390 --> 01:20:29.730
than men, which is why we've
got free swimming given

01:20:29.730 --> 01:20:33.540
by our mayor, all women
over the age of 16.

01:20:33.540 --> 01:20:36.580
My question though is
actually have you got the data

01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:42.210
that breaks this down
into ethnicity in terms

01:20:42.210 --> 01:20:46.020
of healthy life expectancy
that we see on page seven?

01:20:46.020 --> 01:20:49.080
And secondly in terms
of socioeconomic groups

01:20:49.080 --> 01:20:52.020
so that we know the expectation by 2040

01:20:52.020 --> 01:20:56.100
is that healthy life expectancy
will be 20 years difference

01:20:56.100 --> 01:20:57.480
between rich and poor.

01:20:57.480 --> 01:20:59.250
So if you live in a poor area,

01:20:59.250 --> 01:21:03.600
you will expect to hit chronic
disease by the age of 50.

01:21:03.600 --> 01:21:07.320
So have we got this sort
of breakdown of data?

01:21:07.320 --> 01:21:10.770
And finally, I think all
of this data is beginning

01:21:10.770 --> 01:21:15.000
to show us very clearly the
moral cost of not dealing

01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:17.070
with inequalities.

01:21:17.070 --> 01:21:20.190
But do we know what the cost is to the NHS

01:21:20.190 --> 01:21:23.403
of not dealing with these
various inequalities?

01:21:24.480 --> 01:21:27.480
- Okay, were you going to ask
a question? Sarah-Jane? No

01:21:27.480 --> 01:21:31.230
Okay. So just make sure
we try and tackle these.

01:21:31.230 --> 01:21:33.150
I think we've got key thing from Duncan

01:21:33.150 --> 01:21:35.940
on how do we make sure we
weave an inequalities lens

01:21:35.940 --> 01:21:39.480
into all of our ongoing work.

01:21:39.480 --> 01:21:41.430
Maybe focus on that.

01:21:41.430 --> 01:21:46.430
I think the question
on funding strategies,

01:21:46.560 --> 01:21:50.913
Louise's question, I see that
as baked into priority four.

01:21:52.770 --> 01:21:55.170
But there's a question on
all of these six priorities

01:21:55.170 --> 01:21:56.430
about what are the metrics,

01:21:56.430 --> 01:21:58.950
how are we going to know whether
we're making a difference

01:21:58.950 --> 01:22:01.050
and what are we going to look at?

01:22:01.050 --> 01:22:03.090
Which then links into Sam's question

01:22:03.090 --> 01:22:07.230
about have we got more data
that allows us to scrutinise

01:22:07.230 --> 01:22:09.360
in terms of actually probably
all the performance data

01:22:09.360 --> 01:22:12.270
we looked at, how does it
vary by geography, by sex,

01:22:12.270 --> 01:22:17.270
by racial groups, and so on,
a whole load more of that.

01:22:17.640 --> 01:22:20.460
So there's quite a bit on
that all related to data

01:22:20.460 --> 01:22:22.800
and how do we make this
more comprehensive.

01:22:22.800 --> 01:22:25.230
And then we had quite specific questions

01:22:25.230 --> 01:22:30.230
on Louise's question about how
we're thinking about racism.

01:22:30.540 --> 01:22:34.350
Was that in services
or in society or both?

01:22:34.350 --> 01:22:37.560
- [Louise] What's the role
of the NHS to support health

01:22:37.560 --> 01:22:40.260
when racism exists in society?

01:22:40.260 --> 01:22:44.213
'Cause racism impacts on
health and people of colour.

01:22:45.661 --> 01:22:46.494
- Okay.

01:22:47.550 --> 01:22:49.230
In a way that hasn't been picked

01:22:49.230 --> 01:22:51.990
up by what's already been shared

01:22:51.990 --> 01:22:53.010
in terms of what we're doing.

01:22:53.010 --> 01:22:54.450
- [Louise] If people want
to say more about it,

01:22:54.450 --> 01:22:56.160
I'd be grateful.

01:22:56.160 --> 01:22:58.320
- [Penny] Okay, does that cover?

01:22:58.320 --> 01:23:00.660
I think let's try and tackle those.

01:23:00.660 --> 01:23:02.550
Otherwise, we run out of time.

01:23:02.550 --> 01:23:05.610
- So in terms of integrating
health inequalities

01:23:05.610 --> 01:23:10.610
in all that we do, I am
meeting with all the directors

01:23:10.770 --> 01:23:13.560
and working with them to ensure

01:23:13.560 --> 01:23:16.590
that we are sighted on the
work that they are doing,

01:23:16.590 --> 01:23:19.920
that we can input into
what good looks like,

01:23:19.920 --> 01:23:21.780
for reducing inequalities.

01:23:21.780 --> 01:23:23.970
So that's an ongoing piece of work.

01:23:23.970 --> 01:23:27.510
And I've worked, met with
actually several of the directors

01:23:27.510 --> 01:23:29.730
who are owning some of the programmes.

01:23:29.730 --> 01:23:31.050
I've met with them already

01:23:31.050 --> 01:23:35.040
and we've started ensuring
that will happen going forward

01:23:35.040 --> 01:23:37.260
for those who've missed us out.

01:23:37.260 --> 01:23:42.260
In terms of the issue to do
with what the funding flow looks

01:23:43.440 --> 01:23:47.430
like, I think it would be
exceptionally important

01:23:47.430 --> 01:23:52.430
to include deprivation,
explicitly include deprivation.

01:23:52.770 --> 01:23:57.240
When you look at the data on
what is driving inequalities,

01:23:57.240 --> 01:24:00.210
deprivation is a significant factor

01:24:00.210 --> 01:24:03.285
and that needs to be included in a way

01:24:03.285 --> 01:24:05.850
that hasn't previously happened.

01:24:05.850 --> 01:24:08.040
And that needs to be data-driven.

01:24:08.040 --> 01:24:10.950
The last point I was
making was about data.

01:24:10.950 --> 01:24:15.240
If we cannot measure it, if
it has not been highlighted,

01:24:15.240 --> 01:24:20.240
then it is not surfaced for
leaders to take action on.

01:24:20.340 --> 01:24:24.930
If we want to build a coalition
of people who are determined

01:24:24.930 --> 01:24:29.610
to drive down inequalities,
they need to have granular data.

01:24:29.610 --> 01:24:32.220
And that is going to be the
focus of our discussion.

01:24:32.220 --> 01:24:37.220
Ming and I spoke to your team
with David Ashley yesterday.

01:24:37.380 --> 01:24:41.550
If we don't have the data
broken down, granular,

01:24:41.550 --> 01:24:46.550
bringing together community
primary care, secondary care,

01:24:46.920 --> 01:24:49.350
and looking at the whole care pathway,

01:24:49.350 --> 01:24:52.980
we cannot drive improvements.

01:24:52.980 --> 01:24:54.600
We cannot drive improvements

01:24:54.600 --> 01:24:56.250
when we are looking at performance.

01:24:56.250 --> 01:24:59.073
We need the data. It's not negotiable.

01:25:00.286 --> 01:25:01.119
- [Penny] Okay.

01:25:01.119 --> 01:25:02.610
- The point on racism-

01:25:02.610 --> 01:25:03.480
- [Penny] Pick up, please. Yeah.

01:25:03.480 --> 01:25:08.480
- Yeah, sure. So the NHS is
a microcosm of wider society.

01:25:08.670 --> 01:25:11.220
1.3 million people working in the NHS.

01:25:11.220 --> 01:25:12.930
What happens in the wider world is bound

01:25:12.930 --> 01:25:15.420
to be reflected within our workplaces.

01:25:15.420 --> 01:25:17.910
The NHS has a role to play,

01:25:17.910 --> 01:25:20.432
but it cannot be the
only enabler for equity

01:25:20.432 --> 01:25:21.265
and we know that.

01:25:21.265 --> 01:25:23.310
But the role that is playing

01:25:23.310 --> 01:25:27.480
includes setting up the NHS
race and health observatory,

01:25:27.480 --> 01:25:28.980
if I may say that.

01:25:28.980 --> 01:25:32.250
And the work that we are
doing in terms of looking

01:25:32.250 --> 01:25:34.200
at maternal health,

01:25:34.200 --> 01:25:36.900
looking at how we can apply
an anti-racism approach

01:25:36.900 --> 01:25:40.320
to QI methodology, which is
a method that's used a lot

01:25:40.320 --> 01:25:42.427
across the whole of the NHS.

01:25:43.440 --> 01:25:46.320
And then going back to the
point around the cost of racism,

01:25:46.320 --> 01:25:48.600
I think, which was also raised,

01:25:48.600 --> 01:25:50.070
I think that's an important point

01:25:50.070 --> 01:25:55.050
because we need to know
the impact that racism has,

01:25:55.050 --> 01:25:56.790
both in terms of the human cost,

01:25:56.790 --> 01:25:59.880
but also the productivity
and financial cost as well.

01:25:59.880 --> 01:26:00.840
And that's a piece of work

01:26:00.840 --> 01:26:04.080
that we are commissioning later this month

01:26:04.080 --> 01:26:08.070
to really kind of focus on and
to provide even more evidence

01:26:08.070 --> 01:26:11.619
to help kind of focus on the solutions

01:26:11.619 --> 01:26:13.980
to the longstanding
challenges that we have,

01:26:13.980 --> 01:26:14.817
not just in the NHS,

01:26:14.817 --> 01:26:16.917
but of course across the whole of society.

01:26:17.790 --> 01:26:20.430
- Thank you. Okay,
really good conversation.

01:26:20.430 --> 01:26:22.620
Thank you, Dianne, for
taking up this work,

01:26:22.620 --> 01:26:24.307
and thank you, Habib, for joining us,

01:26:24.307 --> 01:26:26.523
and we'll make sure we come back to it.

01:26:27.540 --> 01:26:29.640
I think we have a risk that
coming out of this morning,

01:26:29.640 --> 01:26:34.500
Ming has even more work to do,
but we'll keep going, right?

01:26:34.500 --> 01:26:38.073
Learning disability and autism programme.

01:26:40.530 --> 01:26:42.090
Who's presenting on this?

01:26:42.090 --> 01:26:43.317
- Tom.
- Tom.

01:26:43.317 --> 01:26:44.850
- Hi. Good morning, everyone.

01:26:44.850 --> 01:26:45.683
Tom Cahill,

01:26:45.683 --> 01:26:47.550
National Director of Learning
Disability and Autism,

01:26:47.550 --> 01:26:49.683
former chief exec, not practising anymore.

01:26:50.940 --> 01:26:53.910
Just want to bring you up to
speed on learn disability.

01:26:53.910 --> 01:26:57.303
I have the paper so I won't go
through the paper in detail,

01:26:58.710 --> 01:27:01.950
but we've just finished
talking about inequalities.

01:27:01.950 --> 01:27:03.600
If you've a learning disability,

01:27:03.600 --> 01:27:06.060
the chances are you won't make 50.

01:27:06.060 --> 01:27:07.168
If you're a learninv disability

01:27:07.168 --> 01:27:08.676
with a profound learning disability

01:27:08.676 --> 01:27:10.560
and you're from an ethnic background,

01:27:10.560 --> 01:27:13.410
you won't make 34, 35, 36.

01:27:13.410 --> 01:27:15.600
So have a look around the room

01:27:15.600 --> 01:27:17.550
and how many people would make that life.

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:19.260
So just, I want to bring that home

01:27:19.260 --> 01:27:21.903
that this is about people
and we can do something.

01:27:22.830 --> 01:27:25.530
Learning disability programme
came out of a failure.

01:27:25.530 --> 01:27:28.230
Winterbourne goes back, I'm
sharing this with the board

01:27:28.230 --> 01:27:30.720
'cause I know a lot of
you might be new to this.

01:27:30.720 --> 01:27:34.800
And back in 2015, really,
really difficult circumstances,

01:27:34.800 --> 01:27:36.840
people were treated very poorly.

01:27:36.840 --> 01:27:41.400
Over 2017, 2019 became
part of the long-term plan,

01:27:41.400 --> 01:27:44.520
government strategy around
building right support for one,

01:27:44.520 --> 01:27:45.990
getting people out of hospital,

01:27:45.990 --> 01:27:50.100
but two, helping people live
longer, healthier lives.

01:27:50.100 --> 01:27:51.990
And that was the BTRs.

01:27:51.990 --> 01:27:54.090
Despite the narrative of failure,

01:27:54.090 --> 01:27:55.365
I've been doing this for four years.

01:27:55.365 --> 01:27:56.550
If you go around the country,

01:27:56.550 --> 01:27:59.040
there's some fantastic care and support,

01:27:59.040 --> 01:28:00.630
whether it's through key workers,

01:28:00.630 --> 01:28:03.480
what the people at acute
hospitals are doing,

01:28:03.480 --> 01:28:06.630
seeing people at home before
they come in for an operation.

01:28:06.630 --> 01:28:08.247
There's really good stuff.

01:28:08.247 --> 01:28:10.233
And we need to build
that far more good stuff

01:28:10.233 --> 01:28:12.360
than the raise of failures.

01:28:12.360 --> 01:28:15.753
But we do hear the failures,
and when we do, they're loud.

01:28:16.620 --> 01:28:18.862
Programme is established in 2019

01:28:18.862 --> 01:28:20.111
particularly I talked about,

01:28:20.111 --> 01:28:22.350
and there's two distinct
groups, just to share with you.

01:28:22.350 --> 01:28:24.240
So we've got people with
a learning disability

01:28:24.240 --> 01:28:27.570
and some of them are autistic, about 30%.

01:28:27.570 --> 01:28:30.330
And then you've got a group of
people who are autistic only.

01:28:30.330 --> 01:28:33.120
I'll just deal with those very briefly.

01:28:33.120 --> 01:28:34.337
In terms of learning disability,

01:28:34.337 --> 01:28:38.610
we had two real targets or work to do.

01:28:38.610 --> 01:28:40.623
So getting people out of hospital,

01:28:41.490 --> 01:28:43.560
particularly those who
shouldn't be in hospital,

01:28:43.560 --> 01:28:44.393
not everybody.

01:28:44.393 --> 01:28:45.226
And we'd done some work about
two and a half years ago

01:28:45.226 --> 01:28:49.170
and about 40% we knew that
should not been in hospital.

01:28:49.170 --> 01:28:51.180
We're not convinced that
60% that should be there.

01:28:51.180 --> 01:28:52.410
We're getting the right care either,

01:28:52.410 --> 01:28:54.273
but we need to work on that.

01:28:55.421 --> 01:28:58.650
And we have broadly achieved
that for learning disability,

01:28:58.650 --> 01:29:00.285
not for autistic people.

01:29:00.285 --> 01:29:01.470
And I'll come to that in a second.

01:29:01.470 --> 01:29:03.840
The second part, we were
focusing on health outcomes

01:29:03.840 --> 01:29:05.760
around annual health checks.

01:29:05.760 --> 01:29:10.760
80% target and achieved, but
there's 1.3 million people.

01:29:10.830 --> 01:29:14.070
There's only about 330 on GP registers.

01:29:14.070 --> 01:29:17.260
So you can see that
there's still a big gap

01:29:18.192 --> 01:29:20.523
and that 1.3 million,
there's more we can do.

01:29:22.020 --> 01:29:24.600
If you see on the LeDeR report,

01:29:24.600 --> 01:29:28.860
the median age of death
is improved about a year

01:29:28.860 --> 01:29:32.676
over a seven-year period,
eight year period,

01:29:32.676 --> 01:29:34.634
so really positive.

01:29:34.634 --> 01:29:39.433
And the avoidable deaths
are reduced from 47% to 38%.

01:29:40.590 --> 01:29:41.580
And these are really important

01:29:41.580 --> 01:29:43.320
because these are things we can do.

01:29:43.320 --> 01:29:45.780
And if you think about
where we want to go next,

01:29:45.780 --> 01:29:47.193
we can target that.

01:29:48.210 --> 01:29:52.980
You've then got autistic people
and autistic people only,

01:29:52.980 --> 01:29:55.800
they have a propensity to
experience mental health

01:29:55.800 --> 01:29:57.600
more than the normal population.

01:29:57.600 --> 01:30:00.930
Suicidal ideation, a significant risk.

01:30:00.930 --> 01:30:03.360
And what we are seeing is autistic people,

01:30:03.360 --> 01:30:06.390
young and or old coming into hospital,

01:30:06.390 --> 01:30:08.970
coming into mental health beds,
particularly not into older

01:30:08.970 --> 01:30:11.580
and disability beds as we
might have formally known them.

01:30:11.580 --> 01:30:13.080
And that's a significant increase.

01:30:13.080 --> 01:30:15.720
So we've been working
over the last year to try

01:30:15.720 --> 01:30:18.540
and reduce that, make sure
that the pathways are in place,

01:30:18.540 --> 01:30:20.010
working with mental health teams,

01:30:20.010 --> 01:30:23.790
particularly to make sure
that they're autism friendly.

01:30:23.790 --> 01:30:26.640
And you can see there on paragraph 12,

01:30:26.640 --> 01:30:29.730
it tells you the sort
of work this autism team

01:30:29.730 --> 01:30:31.860
has been doing with across the system.

01:30:31.860 --> 01:30:35.430
Also a lot of research, a lot
of areas of good practise,

01:30:35.430 --> 01:30:37.020
whether it's in Manchester and Stockport

01:30:37.020 --> 01:30:40.350
with Jonathan Greening's work,
whether it's NELFT in London,

01:30:40.350 --> 01:30:41.340
not knowing what they're doing

01:30:41.340 --> 01:30:43.350
around the neurodiversity pathways.

01:30:43.350 --> 01:30:47.130
Some significant work there
and we're seeing progress,

01:30:47.130 --> 01:30:50.610
but equally autistic people
have the same life impacts

01:30:50.610 --> 01:30:52.080
as well about health outcomes.

01:30:52.080 --> 01:30:54.033
So we've got to continue to do that.

01:30:55.290 --> 01:30:58.470
So aligning ourselves
with the 10-year plan,

01:30:58.470 --> 01:31:01.590
real opportunity for
us and for this group.

01:31:01.590 --> 01:31:04.230
Neighbourhoods, you
really get basic and get

01:31:04.230 --> 01:31:07.350
into the local population and
to start making a difference

01:31:07.350 --> 01:31:09.510
and start putting things in place.

01:31:09.510 --> 01:31:12.900
And because of LeDeR, we've
been doing it for eight years,

01:31:12.900 --> 01:31:14.640
we know what we need to do.

01:31:14.640 --> 01:31:16.770
And I'll say a little bit
about that in a second.

01:31:16.770 --> 01:31:20.010
Digital transformation, huge opportunity.

01:31:20.010 --> 01:31:21.750
I'll hear colleagues shouting at me,

01:31:21.750 --> 01:31:23.190
don't forget to tell them about the risks

01:31:23.190 --> 01:31:25.080
because not everyone can accessible.

01:31:25.080 --> 01:31:27.630
But that is something we can work with.

01:31:27.630 --> 01:31:30.870
And Ming, we have people who
got 11 colleagues on our team

01:31:30.870 --> 01:31:34.020
who could be testing for you,
who've got lived experience.

01:31:34.020 --> 01:31:35.403
So happy to do that.

01:31:36.570 --> 01:31:40.110
And clearly prevention programmes
and workforce planning,

01:31:40.110 --> 01:31:41.400
and workforce planning and training.

01:31:41.400 --> 01:31:42.420
And when we talk about training,

01:31:42.420 --> 01:31:45.461
you might be talking about
the Oliver McGowan Training

01:31:45.461 --> 01:31:46.294
and making sure,

01:31:46.294 --> 01:31:48.600
'cause we know that changes
people's perspectives,

01:31:48.600 --> 01:31:51.180
it changes people's
perspectives about people

01:31:51.180 --> 01:31:53.313
who are seen in A&amp;E or wherever.

01:31:54.300 --> 01:31:58.080
So finally from me, chair,
I just, this is doable.

01:31:58.080 --> 01:32:03.080
This is not beyond NHS,
particularly on the NHS term.

01:32:03.510 --> 01:32:06.536
We know there's education,
we know the social care.

01:32:06.536 --> 01:32:08.460
You know, if we do the training for staff,

01:32:08.460 --> 01:32:11.400
if we do the reasonable
adjustments that people need

01:32:11.400 --> 01:32:13.260
to get the right care at the right time.

01:32:13.260 --> 01:32:16.200
We have a digital flag,
which is for disability,

01:32:16.200 --> 01:32:17.760
but is a game changer.

01:32:17.760 --> 01:32:18.960
And if we can use that,

01:32:18.960 --> 01:32:22.460
make sure that we use it
properly on both ends,

01:32:22.460 --> 01:32:25.050
it's completed, and people then,

01:32:25.050 --> 01:32:28.560
when people turn up for
clinics, hospital appointments,

01:32:28.560 --> 01:32:31.860
people understand what the
reasonable adjustments are.

01:32:31.860 --> 01:32:34.143
Vaccinations, particularly flu,

01:32:35.961 --> 01:32:37.290
COVID is less of a concern now.

01:32:37.290 --> 01:32:40.500
Flu and respiratory is a big issue for us.

01:32:40.500 --> 01:32:45.500
Clinical pathways, respiratory,
cancer, CVD, diabetes,

01:32:47.370 --> 01:32:49.980
obesity, the things that we know.

01:32:49.980 --> 01:32:54.030
And if systems and ICBs
together put these in place,

01:32:54.030 --> 01:32:55.980
we can make that difference.

01:32:55.980 --> 01:32:58.770
And clearly annual health
checks for everybody,

01:32:58.770 --> 01:32:59.760
we really indicate,

01:32:59.760 --> 01:33:03.810
and we're talking about
mental health people, SMI,

01:33:03.810 --> 01:33:05.340
we're talking about learning disability,

01:33:05.340 --> 01:33:07.920
and we're talking about
autism, combined health checks,

01:33:07.920 --> 01:33:09.600
and we're quite excited about that.

01:33:09.600 --> 01:33:14.043
So quick canter through,
chair, of the paper.

01:33:14.940 --> 01:33:17.790
But the challenge, I think we
can make a difference here.

01:33:18.900 --> 01:33:19.950
- Thank you, Tom. Very good.

01:33:19.950 --> 01:33:24.750
Good example of what gets
measured gets done. Okay.

01:33:24.750 --> 01:33:28.380
Any questions for Tom? Andrew, yeah.

01:33:28.380 --> 01:33:30.120
- [Andrew] Tom, can I just ask a point

01:33:30.120 --> 01:33:32.010
around the voluntary sector?

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:37.010
So where do you see the voluntary
sector impacting on some

01:33:37.217 --> 01:33:39.843
of the measures that you've outlined?

01:33:42.480 --> 01:33:44.190
- Where we see, we work very closely

01:33:44.190 --> 01:33:46.650
with the voluntary sector
at the moment and they,

01:33:46.650 --> 01:33:48.840
we call them our stakeholders.

01:33:48.840 --> 01:33:50.250
They do a lot of support that's

01:33:50.250 --> 01:33:55.140
probably not on the health
side, but getting people to GPs,

01:33:55.140 --> 01:33:57.420
promoting annual health checks.

01:33:57.420 --> 01:33:59.940
But working alongside is
much more on the social side,

01:33:59.940 --> 01:34:03.780
on the social side of this,
that's where I see that.

01:34:03.780 --> 01:34:04.702
- [Andrew] Okay, thank you.

01:34:04.702 --> 01:34:07.050
- [Penny] Louise. Claire.

01:34:07.050 --> 01:34:07.883
- Thank you.

01:34:07.883 --> 01:34:09.930
So look forward to seeing a kind of,

01:34:09.930 --> 01:34:13.450
or the board discussing a fuller
neurodevelopmental pathway

01:34:14.580 --> 01:34:16.440
piece incorporating the really good work

01:34:16.440 --> 01:34:18.300
of the ADHD task force.

01:34:18.300 --> 01:34:21.663
I've got a question about the
table C and the waiting times,

01:34:23.100 --> 01:34:27.000
and it says, you know,
referrals open at least 13 weeks

01:34:27.000 --> 01:34:31.200
has seen a significant
increase year on year.

01:34:31.200 --> 01:34:32.880
What's the range?

01:34:32.880 --> 01:34:34.650
So at least 13 weeks,

01:34:34.650 --> 01:34:36.690
what's the kind of
higher level of the range

01:34:36.690 --> 01:34:39.263
and how are people being
supported whilst they wait?

01:34:41.730 --> 01:34:44.400
- Chair, honestly, I will
give you the range here.

01:34:44.400 --> 01:34:46.200
Our support, we're
certainly doing a lot of,

01:34:46.200 --> 01:34:49.620
with teams on supporting
people and maintaining well

01:34:49.620 --> 01:34:51.420
while in the pathways,

01:34:51.420 --> 01:34:54.660
doing a lot of work with ADHD task force

01:34:54.660 --> 01:34:59.340
about how can we reduce
those on the pathways,

01:34:59.340 --> 01:35:02.613
but supporting families,
supporting people, reaching out,

01:35:03.540 --> 01:35:05.740
there are some significant
waits, you know,

01:35:06.623 --> 01:35:08.160
and it is something
we're going to deal with.

01:35:08.160 --> 01:35:09.570
I don't think, if we're honest,

01:35:09.570 --> 01:35:11.820
we can deal with it
without additional funding.

01:35:11.820 --> 01:35:14.040
But actually we can't
just stand still either.

01:35:14.040 --> 01:35:17.400
And that's part of the eight-point plan

01:35:17.400 --> 01:35:19.740
that we're working with
Minister Kinnock and colleagues.

01:35:19.740 --> 01:35:23.850
- Yeah, and key bit we
need to come back to, so.

01:35:23.850 --> 01:35:25.453
- I would've thought so.

01:35:26.593 --> 01:35:29.373
So there is a good news
story in there from, and I,

01:35:30.409 --> 01:35:32.790
and sorry, I didn't share it,
the ICBs have been really,

01:35:32.790 --> 01:35:36.870
really working well and
you can see for two months,

01:35:36.870 --> 01:35:39.645
we've levelled off in
terms of the increase

01:35:39.645 --> 01:35:42.453
and the closures.

01:35:42.453 --> 01:35:43.440
They've narrowed that gap.

01:35:43.440 --> 01:35:45.960
So, and that's without additional
resources from the centre.

01:35:45.960 --> 01:35:47.880
So it's really good stuff.

01:35:47.880 --> 01:35:49.710
We need to support that and build on it.

01:35:49.710 --> 01:35:51.803
- Yeah, there's a lot more we need to do.

01:35:51.803 --> 01:35:53.730
Claire, and then I'm going to move us on.

01:35:53.730 --> 01:35:55.227
- Thank you. So thank you, Tom.

01:35:55.227 --> 01:35:58.020
Really good, very clear paper.

01:35:58.020 --> 01:35:58.980
It was just a little bit more

01:35:58.980 --> 01:36:00.180
about the reasonable adjustments

01:36:00.180 --> 01:36:02.670
'cause I think people sort
of go into a little bit

01:36:02.670 --> 01:36:03.780
of a blind panic when they sort

01:36:03.780 --> 01:36:04.920
of think about reasonable adjustments.

01:36:04.920 --> 01:36:07.650
But actually, I mean, and
so I have an autistic child,

01:36:07.650 --> 01:36:11.100
so very familiar with the needs

01:36:11.100 --> 01:36:12.300
and actually what we should think about,

01:36:12.300 --> 01:36:14.370
is rather than designing environments

01:36:14.370 --> 01:36:15.930
that work for autistic people,

01:36:15.930 --> 01:36:17.640
actually just in designing environments,

01:36:17.640 --> 01:36:18.720
if you think about the things,

01:36:18.720 --> 01:36:20.790
the reasonable adjustments
that most people would need

01:36:20.790 --> 01:36:23.280
is don't suddenly change my
appointment at the last minute,

01:36:23.280 --> 01:36:24.750
don't suddenly send me to
a different appointment,

01:36:24.750 --> 01:36:25.583
different room,

01:36:25.583 --> 01:36:27.510
give me information in a way
that I can understand it.

01:36:27.510 --> 01:36:29.250
Let me bring somebody with me,

01:36:29.250 --> 01:36:31.590
and make sure the environment
is as calm as possible.

01:36:31.590 --> 01:36:33.210
So I don't think actually we should focus

01:36:33.210 --> 01:36:34.200
on reasonable adjustments.

01:36:34.200 --> 01:36:35.220
We should talk about actually

01:36:35.220 --> 01:36:38.863
how we design services in a
way that works for people.

01:36:38.863 --> 01:36:40.800
- So we have done quite a bit

01:36:40.800 --> 01:36:42.030
and particularly the autism team

01:36:42.030 --> 01:36:45.060
about what new services should look like,

01:36:45.060 --> 01:36:48.090
and how they're designed, so
inpatient areas, flooring,

01:36:48.090 --> 01:36:52.110
noise, all of the
sensory issues, lighting,

01:36:52.110 --> 01:36:54.120
all of those things are really helpful.

01:36:54.120 --> 01:36:55.710
I think Claire, you're probably,

01:36:55.710 --> 01:36:58.410
it's where we've got existing facilities,

01:36:58.410 --> 01:36:59.430
how do we work for those?

01:36:59.430 --> 01:37:02.220
But it isn't just as you said,
it's not just environment.

01:37:02.220 --> 01:37:04.440
It's about how do we treat people.

01:37:04.440 --> 01:37:06.600
- Okay, great. Right,
we're going to move us on.

01:37:06.600 --> 01:37:08.910
So management and leadership development.

01:37:08.910 --> 01:37:12.990
Jo, Carolyn, I assume it's been read.

01:37:12.990 --> 01:37:15.300
- Yes, I will. I'll be
really quick, thank you.

01:37:15.300 --> 01:37:16.560
Just two things. So thank you.

01:37:16.560 --> 01:37:18.090
We're seeking the board's review

01:37:18.090 --> 01:37:22.260
and approval of the management
leadership framework today,

01:37:22.260 --> 01:37:24.330
which basically for the first time means

01:37:24.330 --> 01:37:27.120
that there'll be a clear
set of consistent standards

01:37:27.120 --> 01:37:30.140
and expectations of managing
needs at all levels.

01:37:30.140 --> 01:37:33.510
So from entry level right to
board level, which, you know,

01:37:33.510 --> 01:37:35.430
incredibly we've never had before.

01:37:35.430 --> 01:37:39.556
So we are at the start of
very long overdue journey.

01:37:39.556 --> 01:37:41.412
The reason why we've got where
we are is not thanks to me,

01:37:41.412 --> 01:37:42.270
thanks to people like Carolyn May

01:37:42.270 --> 01:37:44.730
and the team who've
done work with thousands

01:37:44.730 --> 01:37:46.443
of people across the system working

01:37:46.443 --> 01:37:48.930
with the Chartered Management Institute,

01:37:48.930 --> 01:37:50.850
and lots of people that
Carolyn can tell you

01:37:50.850 --> 01:37:53.040
about to get to where we've got today.

01:37:53.040 --> 01:37:56.520
So as say, is the beginning of
a journey, it's not perfect.

01:37:56.520 --> 01:37:57.480
I will say that.

01:37:57.480 --> 01:38:00.060
I think there's what,
were many sort of refines

01:38:00.060 --> 01:38:03.390
in the language as the
10-year plan evolves,

01:38:03.390 --> 01:38:04.617
I think our notion of what management

01:38:04.617 --> 01:38:06.630
and leadership is required will change

01:38:06.630 --> 01:38:10.230
and we need to keep that
under constant review.

01:38:10.230 --> 01:38:12.870
It's a foundation that
we can start to build on,

01:38:12.870 --> 01:38:14.490
and develop from there.

01:38:14.490 --> 01:38:16.680
And then secondly, if
the board approves that,

01:38:16.680 --> 01:38:18.930
then in the light of that, we
need to align the standards

01:38:18.930 --> 01:38:21.510
with the board competency framework.

01:38:21.510 --> 01:38:24.180
And so we're recommending in '26 and '27,

01:38:24.180 --> 01:38:26.520
we use this new leadership framework

01:38:26.520 --> 01:38:30.180
for board level managers and leaders.

01:38:30.180 --> 01:38:32.340
So, take questions.

01:38:32.340 --> 01:38:33.540
- Okay, let's take questions.

01:38:33.540 --> 01:38:38.540
So David, Paul, and Robert, and Ming.

01:38:41.700 --> 01:38:44.340
Okay, take all those, we'll
take all the questions together.

01:38:44.340 --> 01:38:45.173
So David.

01:38:46.410 --> 01:38:50.490
- I welcome this approach.
I would make three points.

01:38:50.490 --> 01:38:54.000
One, hopefully it has both developmental

01:38:54.000 --> 01:38:58.710
and assessment aspects to
it, performance assessment.

01:38:58.710 --> 01:38:59.820
On assessment,

01:38:59.820 --> 01:39:03.120
I would observe that to do
this sort of thing well,

01:39:03.120 --> 01:39:05.820
it's very resource intensive.

01:39:05.820 --> 01:39:10.820
So for example, if you are
evaluating the performance

01:39:10.890 --> 01:39:13.140
of someone ideally that is not just done

01:39:13.140 --> 01:39:16.530
by their line manager where
all sorts of issues arise.

01:39:16.530 --> 01:39:20.250
The moment you have a group of
people assessing individuals,

01:39:20.250 --> 01:39:22.550
then it starts to get
very resource intensive.

01:39:24.990 --> 01:39:28.413
Secondly, an assessment, sorry.

01:39:29.670 --> 01:39:31.593
And then finally on development,

01:39:33.210 --> 01:39:36.730
we know that the best way
to learn is on the job

01:39:37.680 --> 01:39:40.173
by observing other people
who do things well.

01:39:41.134 --> 01:39:45.330
And so coaching I think needs
a lot of emphasis in terms

01:39:45.330 --> 01:39:47.280
of the performance indicators

01:39:47.280 --> 01:39:50.343
for line managers all
the way up the chain.

01:39:51.630 --> 01:39:52.867
- Okay, let's keep going round,

01:39:52.867 --> 01:39:55.560
'cause we might find we've
got some common themes.

01:39:55.560 --> 01:39:56.393
Paul.

01:39:57.540 --> 01:39:59.550
- And linking to David's point

01:39:59.550 --> 01:40:02.853
about how to make this improvement happen.

01:40:04.560 --> 01:40:06.960
85% of people had appraisal

01:40:06.960 --> 01:40:09.783
and 26% said it improved their job.

01:40:11.730 --> 01:40:16.540
So appraisal plays very well at the moment

01:40:17.790 --> 01:40:21.570
and therefore given that by
April next year, we are going

01:40:21.570 --> 01:40:25.560
to be wanting managers to
manage a great deal of change.

01:40:25.560 --> 01:40:28.350
The nature of appraisal
from then will have

01:40:28.350 --> 01:40:29.883
to be quite a lot better.

01:40:31.770 --> 01:40:35.100
And so therefore the
developmental programme

01:40:35.100 --> 01:40:37.270
to develop appraisal by next April

01:40:38.280 --> 01:40:39.903
better get going quite quickly.

01:40:42.030 --> 01:40:46.080
Otherwise, and since coming
back to my very first point

01:40:46.080 --> 01:40:50.103
in this board about
change, you lose a year.

01:40:52.950 --> 01:40:55.590
So given we're going to need managers

01:40:55.590 --> 01:40:58.260
to be managing difficult things,

01:40:58.260 --> 01:41:01.173
the appraisal of those
managers needs to get going,

01:41:02.027 --> 01:41:05.670
and as David said, it
is resource intensive,

01:41:05.670 --> 01:41:09.020
but the loss of resource
of having only 26%

01:41:09.020 --> 01:41:12.063
of people improving their
performance is gigantic.

01:41:14.127 --> 01:41:15.877
- [Penny] Robert.

01:41:15.877 --> 01:41:18.000
- I mean this is just so mission critical.

01:41:18.000 --> 01:41:22.440
It's crucial and I think
you're doing a great job.

01:41:22.440 --> 01:41:25.620
My question really is around the college

01:41:25.620 --> 01:41:26.790
that's being established,

01:41:26.790 --> 01:41:30.870
whether appropriate uses
being made of skills

01:41:30.870 --> 01:41:33.480
from outside the NHS from other sectors

01:41:33.480 --> 01:41:38.010
and also whether business
schools in universities,

01:41:38.010 --> 01:41:40.470
some of which are very
motivated to engage with this.

01:41:40.470 --> 01:41:43.080
I know the one at King's is for example,

01:41:43.080 --> 01:41:46.143
whether that is being drawn in to help.

01:41:47.970 --> 01:41:50.163
- Okay. And then Ming.

01:41:51.750 --> 01:41:53.730
- Thanks, Penny.

01:41:53.730 --> 01:41:57.060
Jo, I just wanted to link
the last two conversations.

01:41:57.060 --> 01:41:58.620
So around learning disabilities

01:41:58.620 --> 01:42:03.407
'cause I kind of chair
the neurodiversity network

01:42:05.070 --> 01:42:07.410
and quite often a lot of the conditions

01:42:07.410 --> 01:42:10.770
that we create at work are not great,

01:42:10.770 --> 01:42:13.110
and line managers haven't
been given that support.

01:42:13.110 --> 01:42:14.943
So I just wanted to make sure

01:42:14.943 --> 01:42:16.543
in any new programme that we did

01:42:16.543 --> 01:42:18.990
that we do consider those kind

01:42:18.990 --> 01:42:22.323
of environmental things as well.

01:42:23.789 --> 01:42:25.830
- Okay, and Andrew was
whispering in my ear

01:42:25.830 --> 01:42:28.650
whether this applies to NHS
England as well as providers.

01:42:28.650 --> 01:42:32.640
- So I think every employer
should guarantee an appraisal.

01:42:32.640 --> 01:42:35.130
We should be saying every year,

01:42:35.130 --> 01:42:37.350
you should have a
conversation with your boss.

01:42:37.350 --> 01:42:40.650
We have failed to do that in
NHS England comprehensively,

01:42:40.650 --> 01:42:41.850
unfortunately.

01:42:41.850 --> 01:42:45.540
So if we're implementing this,

01:42:45.540 --> 01:42:47.640
we should apply it to
our own organisation.

01:42:47.640 --> 01:42:48.870
- We're going for it through
at the moment, Andrew,

01:42:48.870 --> 01:42:50.940
every minute of every day.

01:42:50.940 --> 01:42:54.635
- Okay, I'm going to have Mark and Glen,

01:42:54.635 --> 01:42:55.468
and then I'll add a few things,

01:42:55.468 --> 01:42:57.810
then we'll come back
to you, Jo and Carolyn,

01:42:57.810 --> 01:43:00.180
including what do we do
next, which I recognise.

01:43:00.180 --> 01:43:04.113
- So this is great thinking,

01:43:07.140 --> 01:43:09.810
having just been through
a five-year journey

01:43:09.810 --> 01:43:11.163
of something like this.

01:43:12.150 --> 01:43:16.770
Embedding is always the
almost impossible task.

01:43:16.770 --> 01:43:21.570
And the reflection I have
if I look past is we started

01:43:21.570 --> 01:43:23.403
off with something this long,

01:43:24.390 --> 01:43:27.250
literally this long and we
ended up with eight words

01:43:28.470 --> 01:43:31.020
and this is impossible to implement.

01:43:31.020 --> 01:43:32.370
So the thinking is amazing.

01:43:32.370 --> 01:43:34.800
I think the next task
is how you synthesise it

01:43:34.800 --> 01:43:39.060
down to something that's
repeatably consumable

01:43:39.060 --> 01:43:41.340
by people at every single level.

01:43:41.340 --> 01:43:43.957
And it's the sort of
Winston Churchill quote,

01:43:43.957 --> 01:43:46.740
"If I had more time, I'd
write you a shorter letter."

01:43:46.740 --> 01:43:48.890
I think you probably
need a shorter letter.

01:43:50.610 --> 01:43:51.510
- Glen.

01:43:51.510 --> 01:43:53.490
- Yeah, I agree with
Mark's comment on that,

01:43:53.490 --> 01:43:55.410
but I think it is a great framework.

01:43:55.410 --> 01:43:59.160
I think it concentrates
on how we develop people

01:43:59.160 --> 01:44:00.810
that we have.

01:44:00.810 --> 01:44:02.420
I wonder whether we need to look more

01:44:02.420 --> 01:44:04.410
at values-based recruitment

01:44:04.410 --> 01:44:06.090
and to ensure that we have people

01:44:06.090 --> 01:44:08.163
that we can more easily develop.

01:44:10.140 --> 01:44:11.010
- Yeah, good point.

01:44:11.010 --> 01:44:15.480
Okay, well, can I, I've got
a few just to add as well,

01:44:15.480 --> 01:44:16.590
which I think very much build

01:44:16.590 --> 01:44:18.000
on what other people have said.

01:44:18.000 --> 01:44:23.000
So I have to say I was very
much where David and Paul was,

01:44:23.580 --> 01:44:27.570
it felt to me like this needs
an evaluation framework.

01:44:27.570 --> 01:44:30.390
So I think it's great the
starting point, the stage one,

01:44:30.390 --> 01:44:32.670
stage two, stage three, stage four,

01:44:32.670 --> 01:44:35.940
but that almost says this
is what we think you need.

01:44:35.940 --> 01:44:40.200
It doesn't say this is what
sub acceptable performance

01:44:40.200 --> 01:44:42.510
would look like, this is
what good performance,

01:44:42.510 --> 01:44:43.590
this is what very good,

01:44:43.590 --> 01:44:45.600
and this is what exceptional
performance would look like.

01:44:45.600 --> 01:44:47.880
And I think we need that
for all of these bits

01:44:47.880 --> 01:44:49.500
that would make it longer.

01:44:49.500 --> 01:44:52.980
But that would almost be
the implementation piece

01:44:52.980 --> 01:44:55.380
to support colleagues across the system.

01:44:55.380 --> 01:44:57.000
So if we're going to do it,

01:44:57.000 --> 01:44:59.790
it would need something that embeds that,

01:44:59.790 --> 01:45:04.290
that then if we did that well
ought to take the 20% up,

01:45:04.290 --> 01:45:06.240
because actually it ought
to then give people,

01:45:06.240 --> 01:45:09.510
even looking at an evaluation
framework tells you

01:45:09.510 --> 01:45:11.160
this is what really good looks like.

01:45:11.160 --> 01:45:13.871
And so it gives you a bit
of something to aspire to,

01:45:13.871 --> 01:45:15.150
or a learning curve.

01:45:15.150 --> 01:45:18.120
It feels like we need a lot more on that.

01:45:18.120 --> 01:45:19.500
I think there is this crucial bit

01:45:19.500 --> 01:45:20.760
about the developmental bit

01:45:20.760 --> 01:45:22.830
that David raised and then Robert raised.

01:45:22.830 --> 01:45:27.830
So how we bringing much more
external expertise into this.

01:45:27.994 --> 01:45:30.780
You know, we know that we
have some great management

01:45:30.780 --> 01:45:32.070
expertise in the NHS.

01:45:32.070 --> 01:45:35.910
We know that some skill sets
that we are missing and lacking

01:45:35.910 --> 01:45:40.910
and I think we don't quite
bring enough of that into it.

01:45:41.100 --> 01:45:43.560
And then I have to add a
bit of a personal problem

01:45:43.560 --> 01:45:45.120
with some of the language in here.

01:45:45.120 --> 01:45:49.950
So you know, is one on self-effectiveness,

01:45:49.950 --> 01:45:53.940
keep safe, what does that mean?

01:45:53.940 --> 01:45:57.813
Does that mean I walk
slowly down the corridor or,

01:45:59.160 --> 01:46:01.230
and we keep using that word,

01:46:01.230 --> 01:46:02.830
I wouldn't know what that meant.

01:46:04.320 --> 01:46:07.443
And we've got things in here
like patient-centered care.

01:46:08.460 --> 01:46:09.450
I dunno what that means.

01:46:09.450 --> 01:46:11.550
We've got a whole lot of
really good patient experience

01:46:11.550 --> 01:46:13.740
metrics which we could be aspiring to.

01:46:13.740 --> 01:46:16.770
So we've got a bit too
much fuzzy language in here

01:46:16.770 --> 01:46:20.310
and I think we need to be
much clearer on the sorts

01:46:20.310 --> 01:46:21.660
of things we've been talking about today,

01:46:21.660 --> 01:46:23.790
like do we have a group of managers

01:46:23.790 --> 01:46:25.920
who can really think
about resource allocation?

01:46:25.920 --> 01:46:26.873
We do refer to that,

01:46:26.873 --> 01:46:29.400
but I'm not sure it's quite tight enough.

01:46:29.400 --> 01:46:31.800
Are we really thinking
about great user experience?

01:46:31.800 --> 01:46:32.633
What's that mean?

01:46:32.633 --> 01:46:35.160
Are we really thinking
about this point Paul made

01:46:35.160 --> 01:46:37.740
at the beginning about
the risks of not changing,

01:46:37.740 --> 01:46:39.720
sometimes greater than
the risks of change?

01:46:39.720 --> 01:46:44.490
So I think it'd be good
to hear your thoughts

01:46:44.490 --> 01:46:46.980
on how do we make this much more,

01:46:46.980 --> 01:46:50.130
how do we embed evaluation
throughout this?

01:46:50.130 --> 01:46:51.750
How are we thinking about development

01:46:51.750 --> 01:46:56.340
that goes alongside this,
including in external expertise?

01:46:56.340 --> 01:46:59.610
How can we tighten up some of the language

01:46:59.610 --> 01:47:04.050
and then what do you want to do with this?

01:47:04.050 --> 01:47:08.460
So do we want to roll it out
as it is recognising some

01:47:08.460 --> 01:47:11.040
of the questions, concerns,

01:47:11.040 --> 01:47:13.440
or do you want to do a bit more work on it

01:47:13.440 --> 01:47:15.505
and then roll it out?

01:47:16.897 --> 01:47:18.080
- There's quite a lot there, Penny.

01:47:18.080 --> 01:47:19.522
So just I'll come in on some of those,

01:47:19.522 --> 01:47:21.330
and then I'll ask you to
come back into the specifics,

01:47:21.330 --> 01:47:23.280
then I'll wrap up in
terms of the next steps.

01:47:23.280 --> 01:47:25.860
But yeah, I completely
agree with you, Glen,

01:47:25.860 --> 01:47:27.540
about values-based recruitment.

01:47:27.540 --> 01:47:30.000
If we want to change the
culture within our workforce

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:32.730
and actually it's the
people that we recruit,

01:47:32.730 --> 01:47:34.110
and the people who join the NHS tend

01:47:34.110 --> 01:47:35.790
to be much more values-based anyway

01:47:35.790 --> 01:47:37.820
and the younger generation even more so.

01:47:37.820 --> 01:47:38.970
So I think we need to reflect

01:47:38.970 --> 01:47:42.390
that in the 10-year workforce
plan that we're developing

01:47:42.390 --> 01:47:44.880
and absolutely marking, Penny, we need to,

01:47:44.880 --> 01:47:45.840
it's fantastic work.

01:47:45.840 --> 01:47:47.880
I think it's been very
much facing the people

01:47:47.880 --> 01:47:50.850
who've been developing
it and we need to flip it

01:47:50.850 --> 01:47:53.580
and make it, as you say,
succinct and meaningful to those

01:47:53.580 --> 01:47:57.260
who are going to use it and
talk about it drawing on all

01:47:57.260 --> 01:47:58.860
of that great work.

01:47:58.860 --> 01:48:02.670
I think it is definitely
meant to be developmental

01:48:02.670 --> 01:48:04.890
and I might ask explain
sort of how we're doing

01:48:04.890 --> 01:48:08.250
that and how we're involving
other sectors in that.

01:48:08.250 --> 01:48:09.083
- Yeah, certainly.

01:48:09.083 --> 01:48:11.100
Thank you all very much
for your questions.

01:48:11.100 --> 01:48:13.590
So absolutely this is a
developmental framework

01:48:13.590 --> 01:48:14.970
and the intention is to launch

01:48:14.970 --> 01:48:19.470
with a self-assessment toolkit, excuse me.

01:48:19.470 --> 01:48:22.920
And then a 360-degree feedback
tool next year picking

01:48:22.920 --> 01:48:24.810
up on David's point around feedback

01:48:24.810 --> 01:48:28.770
from other people recognising
it's in resource intensive

01:48:28.770 --> 01:48:32.190
behind this then sits a
national development curriculum

01:48:32.190 --> 01:48:34.020
linking to the code and standards

01:48:34.020 --> 01:48:36.690
which will guide development in terms

01:48:36.690 --> 01:48:39.390
of the core competencies
and expectations for leaders

01:48:39.390 --> 01:48:41.400
and managers at different levels.

01:48:41.400 --> 01:48:44.670
I think the key point
is we've engaged hugely

01:48:44.670 --> 01:48:46.020
on this piece of work.

01:48:46.020 --> 01:48:48.150
So over a thousand people have fed in.

01:48:48.150 --> 01:48:51.120
So of course sometimes
the language isn't exactly

01:48:51.120 --> 01:48:53.640
what everyone would want and
it's been a balance to try

01:48:53.640 --> 01:48:57.180
to get everyone kind of
satisfied with the feedback

01:48:57.180 --> 01:48:58.650
that's been given.

01:48:58.650 --> 01:49:02.880
Just on the point about external
guidance and development

01:49:02.880 --> 01:49:05.190
and learning from other sectors as part

01:49:05.190 --> 01:49:07.140
of that national development curriculum,

01:49:07.140 --> 01:49:11.190
lots of that is featured there
and case studies and learning

01:49:11.190 --> 01:49:14.193
and how people can apply
that learning in the NHS.

01:49:15.060 --> 01:49:15.990
Excuse me.

01:49:15.990 --> 01:49:17.610
The other thing I just
wanted to pick up, Penny,

01:49:17.610 --> 01:49:19.620
with your point about what does good

01:49:19.620 --> 01:49:21.030
and not so good look like.

01:49:21.030 --> 01:49:23.490
That's exactly what we've
had actually developed

01:49:23.490 --> 01:49:24.990
for the code of practise in terms

01:49:24.990 --> 01:49:27.000
of what's really good
behaviour and values,

01:49:27.000 --> 01:49:28.170
what does that look like?

01:49:28.170 --> 01:49:29.850
What's unacceptable?

01:49:29.850 --> 01:49:32.760
And the plan is to publish
that alongside the framework

01:49:32.760 --> 01:49:35.460
so people know what one
line managers know what good

01:49:35.460 --> 01:49:37.290
and not so good looks like.

01:49:37.290 --> 01:49:40.470
Just on the appraisal,
yeah, really important.

01:49:40.470 --> 01:49:43.230
The very next step for us is
working with a steering group

01:49:43.230 --> 01:49:46.530
to develop that appraisal core framework.

01:49:46.530 --> 01:49:48.180
But we also think training

01:49:48.180 --> 01:49:50.970
for line managers is going to
be absolutely critical there

01:49:50.970 --> 01:49:52.830
'cause this is a kind of cultural change

01:49:52.830 --> 01:49:56.760
about how to do appraisal really
well in a motivational way.

01:49:56.760 --> 01:49:58.553
Apologies for my voice.

01:49:58.553 --> 01:49:59.880
- Carolyn's just getting
over tonsillitis Sorry.

01:49:59.880 --> 01:50:01.680
So she's been really good to come in,

01:50:01.680 --> 01:50:04.230
but I think you're good about
timing, Penny, is really key.

01:50:04.230 --> 01:50:07.110
We were planning to launch this in autumn

01:50:07.110 --> 01:50:10.350
so I think it's how quickly
we can sort of take it offline

01:50:10.350 --> 01:50:12.870
and just address and finesse
particularly the language

01:50:12.870 --> 01:50:16.110
and the succinctness
without losing the pace.

01:50:16.110 --> 01:50:18.477
I'd like to think we could
still sort of crack on,

01:50:18.477 --> 01:50:20.730
but I think it'd be a problem
if it went beyond autumn,

01:50:20.730 --> 01:50:22.780
wouldn't it, in terms of
what we were trying to do.

01:50:22.780 --> 01:50:25.530
- Well, it delays because the
intention is to ask people

01:50:25.530 --> 01:50:27.630
to self-assess against
the framework as part

01:50:27.630 --> 01:50:29.250
of the next appraisal round.

01:50:29.250 --> 01:50:31.440
So obviously we need a
kind of final version

01:50:31.440 --> 01:50:32.940
of the framework and final version

01:50:32.940 --> 01:50:34.600
of the appraisal framework
for them to do that.

01:50:34.600 --> 01:50:36.420
- Okay, and Andrew's reminding me,

01:50:36.420 --> 01:50:40.320
which I was involved in
that when we had the chairs

01:50:40.320 --> 01:50:44.310
and the NEDs framework, it
went down like a lead balloon.

01:50:44.310 --> 01:50:48.600
- So I think the trick is
to take up Mark's point

01:50:48.600 --> 01:50:52.900
around making this succinct
and giving some latitude

01:50:53.790 --> 01:50:58.020
for boards around, you know,
just doing it your way,

01:50:58.020 --> 01:50:59.790
'cause everyone will want

01:50:59.790 --> 01:51:03.000
to put their own kind of spin on this.

01:51:03.000 --> 01:51:06.690
So, you know, we are
offering a way of doing this

01:51:06.690 --> 01:51:11.040
that's proven to work, but
you may have situations

01:51:11.040 --> 01:51:14.430
where a board particularly
wants it done in, you know,

01:51:14.430 --> 01:51:15.930
X, Y, Z way.

01:51:15.930 --> 01:51:19.560
And I think we should
give people that latitude.

01:51:19.560 --> 01:51:23.130
And last time round this,
this didn't go down well

01:51:23.130 --> 01:51:26.430
with some people, but I think if we try

01:51:26.430 --> 01:51:28.443
and make it succinct as possible,

01:51:29.520 --> 01:51:31.980
we hopefully will be a lot more successful

01:51:31.980 --> 01:51:34.380
'cause there's some
really good stuff in here.

01:51:34.380 --> 01:51:37.740
- Okay, so can I make a suggestion?

01:51:37.740 --> 01:51:42.513
Here I go. So it'll still
be autumn in November.

01:51:46.087 --> 01:51:46.920
So-

01:51:53.490 --> 01:51:55.530
So we've got a couple of months.

01:51:55.530 --> 01:51:58.740
I think there's a lot of
