if this went out as it
is, chief people officers

01:52:51.780 --> 01:52:54.540
and organisations
generally will realign all

01:52:54.540 --> 01:52:56.910
of their appraisal
documentation and the frameworks

01:52:56.910 --> 01:52:59.160
and there's quite a lot
of work involved in that.

01:52:59.160 --> 01:53:00.540
Some of 'em have just done it,

01:53:00.540 --> 01:53:02.790
some of 'em have been an
established partner being happy

01:53:02.790 --> 01:53:04.710
with it, et cetera.

01:53:04.710 --> 01:53:07.407
So on your point, Andrew,
I dunno if you've done it,

01:53:07.407 --> 01:53:11.940
but just it's worth talking
to some colleagues out there

01:53:11.940 --> 01:53:14.790
about what would take to
actually bring this to life

01:53:14.790 --> 01:53:16.710
without everybody tearing everything up.

01:53:16.710 --> 01:53:17.792
- Yeah.

01:53:17.792 --> 01:53:18.690
- Starting again from scratch

01:53:18.690 --> 01:53:20.820
and that actually then taking longer.

01:53:20.820 --> 01:53:21.653
- Yeah.

01:53:21.653 --> 01:53:22.800
- Just to come back on that, Jim.

01:53:22.800 --> 01:53:26.040
So we're engaging with over
130 organisations in terms

01:53:26.040 --> 01:53:28.410
of early adoption and we're already doing

01:53:28.410 --> 01:53:31.589
that engagement piece about
how do we implement this well

01:53:31.589 --> 01:53:33.240
and work with them with
community to practise.

01:53:33.240 --> 01:53:34.343
So, thank you.

01:53:34.343 --> 01:53:38.070
- Okay, so are you okay
with what suggested

01:53:38.070 --> 01:53:39.720
in terms of next steps, Jim?

01:53:39.720 --> 01:53:40.872
- Yep.

01:53:40.872 --> 01:53:43.080
- Okay. Alright, good.

01:53:43.080 --> 01:53:48.080
Right, now onto, covering
an awful lot today.

01:53:48.360 --> 01:53:51.800
Greener NHS. David.

01:53:51.800 --> 01:53:52.633
- So thanks, Chair.

01:53:52.633 --> 01:53:54.960
So I'll hand over to Chris in a moment,

01:53:54.960 --> 01:53:56.730
but just say a couple of
very, very quick things.

01:53:56.730 --> 01:53:59.580
Firstly, delighted that
we've got this report

01:53:59.580 --> 01:54:01.200
in public board.

01:54:01.200 --> 01:54:03.600
This is part of the delivering
the net zero strategy.

01:54:03.600 --> 01:54:05.820
And I suppose as you'll see in the report,

01:54:05.820 --> 01:54:08.370
it presents good examples in two things.

01:54:08.370 --> 01:54:11.970
One, the moral obligation to
get involved with this work,

01:54:11.970 --> 01:54:15.390
which the NHS has and you'll
see, you'll hear from Chris,

01:54:15.390 --> 01:54:16.710
has done incredibly well.

01:54:16.710 --> 01:54:18.750
Secondly, and highlight
throughout the report,

01:54:18.750 --> 01:54:21.330
genuine cost savings and
efficiency as a result

01:54:21.330 --> 01:54:22.860
of being involved in this.

01:54:22.860 --> 01:54:24.990
And then finally, and I can say this,

01:54:24.990 --> 01:54:26.880
as a hospital chief executive,

01:54:26.880 --> 01:54:30.120
this stuff really matters to our staff.

01:54:30.120 --> 01:54:32.880
So there is a third benefit
from the good work that Chris

01:54:32.880 --> 01:54:35.297
and his colleagues have left, Chris .

01:54:37.260 --> 01:54:39.414
- Thank you, David. Thank you, Chair.

01:54:39.414 --> 01:54:42.390
I'm delighted to present
to the board today,

01:54:42.390 --> 01:54:44.460
both an improved understanding

01:54:44.460 --> 01:54:46.377
of our carbon footprint in the NHS,

01:54:46.377 --> 01:54:48.570
but also a report setting out progress

01:54:48.570 --> 01:54:49.983
over the last five years.

01:54:51.030 --> 01:54:54.120
The headline figures are a 16% reduction

01:54:54.120 --> 01:54:55.410
in our carbon footprints.

01:54:55.410 --> 01:54:58.260
So those are emissions
we can directly control.

01:54:58.260 --> 01:55:00.300
And for those emissions
that we can influence,

01:55:00.300 --> 01:55:02.190
having seen them go up
during the pandemic,

01:55:02.190 --> 01:55:03.510
we've actually seen them return

01:55:03.510 --> 01:55:07.410
to broadly pre pandemic levels.

01:55:07.410 --> 01:55:09.963
And that's despite an
increase in activity.

01:55:11.550 --> 01:55:13.560
Those improvements across
our carbon footprints

01:55:13.560 --> 01:55:15.360
are across our estate,
they're across our fleet,

01:55:15.360 --> 01:55:17.880
they're across the medicines we use,

01:55:17.880 --> 01:55:19.200
and they're delivered in partnership.

01:55:19.200 --> 01:55:22.050
So really crucially through our colleagues

01:55:22.050 --> 01:55:25.890
in royal colleges, through
the government departments

01:55:25.890 --> 01:55:28.069
and through our suppliers as well.

01:55:28.069 --> 01:55:32.310
And if I just take a
couple of notable actions

01:55:32.310 --> 01:55:34.950
out of the progress report,

01:55:34.950 --> 01:55:37.260
we've virtually eliminated the use

01:55:37.260 --> 01:55:40.350
of certain anaesthetic gases

01:55:40.350 --> 01:55:43.830
with high global warming
potential such as desflurane.

01:55:43.830 --> 01:55:46.170
We've seen huge innovation in our fleet,

01:55:46.170 --> 01:55:47.850
we've delivered the
world's first double crewed

01:55:47.850 --> 01:55:51.690
electric ambulance and more
recently we've partnered

01:55:51.690 --> 01:55:56.690
with great British energy to
invest over a hundred million

01:55:58.140 --> 01:56:01.170
to deliver a triple increase in the amount

01:56:01.170 --> 01:56:03.540
of solar generation across our estate.

01:56:03.540 --> 01:56:06.210
That's going to pay back three times over,

01:56:06.210 --> 01:56:08.580
and that will add to the 5 million pounds

01:56:08.580 --> 01:56:10.110
that we already see in savings each year

01:56:10.110 --> 01:56:11.580
through existing solar.

01:56:11.580 --> 01:56:14.373
So that's just a two or three examples.

01:56:15.420 --> 01:56:16.620
There are many more really,

01:56:16.620 --> 01:56:18.753
really good examples
throughout the progress report

01:56:18.753 --> 01:56:20.373
that we seek to publish today.

01:56:21.480 --> 01:56:24.750
And much of that delivery
against our ambitions is really

01:56:24.750 --> 01:56:27.120
through the incredible efforts

01:56:27.120 --> 01:56:29.790
and dedication of staff
right across the NHS.

01:56:29.790 --> 01:56:31.080
I'd just like to take this opportunity

01:56:31.080 --> 01:56:35.670
to say a big thank you to them
for their continued effort

01:56:35.670 --> 01:56:38.370
and support to deliver greener NHS.

01:56:38.370 --> 01:56:39.903
Thank you.

01:56:39.903 --> 01:56:44.903
- Take questions, comments.
Sam, Duncan, Ravi.

01:56:47.700 --> 01:56:49.230
Sam.

01:56:49.230 --> 01:56:52.860
- Yes, if you look at
some systems, some areas,

01:56:52.860 --> 01:56:56.940
advice and guidance can
reduce travel to outpatients

01:56:56.940 --> 01:56:58.950
by up to 60%.

01:56:58.950 --> 01:57:00.600
And in some systems, guess what?

01:57:00.600 --> 01:57:01.950
You don't need to go to outpatients

01:57:01.950 --> 01:57:03.480
before or after to have your blood tests.

01:57:03.480 --> 01:57:05.940
You can get them done in primary care.

01:57:05.940 --> 01:57:09.160
My question underlying
this is to what extent

01:57:10.050 --> 01:57:13.950
are you looking at or are policy
documents in this instance,

01:57:13.950 --> 01:57:16.380
particularly on
transformation of outpatients

01:57:16.380 --> 01:57:20.220
to look at some of these
potential green gains?

01:57:20.220 --> 01:57:25.220
And the second question is if
you sit in a general practise,

01:57:26.400 --> 01:57:28.410
you'll have a popup.

01:57:28.410 --> 01:57:30.930
So we're doing great things on inhalers,

01:57:30.930 --> 01:57:32.550
but you'll see a popup saying you have

01:57:32.550 --> 01:57:37.550
to have a new plastic spacer every year.

01:57:38.370 --> 01:57:40.680
And actually when there was
a parliamentary question

01:57:40.680 --> 01:57:41.513
about this,

01:57:41.513 --> 01:57:45.870
it was very unclear as to
who's driven this process,

01:57:45.870 --> 01:57:47.433
but it costs millions.

01:57:48.330 --> 01:57:50.910
But hidden within that question

01:57:50.910 --> 01:57:54.510
is also what you have talked about, David,

01:57:54.510 --> 01:57:56.610
which is about engaging people out there.

01:57:56.610 --> 01:58:00.000
This is very engaging
for a lot of clinicians

01:58:00.000 --> 01:58:02.010
and staff at the NHS,

01:58:02.010 --> 01:58:04.980
but to what extent do we engage
'em on a day-to-day basis

01:58:04.980 --> 01:58:08.610
and do they see their
ability to reach the top

01:58:08.610 --> 01:58:12.360
of the organisation and
see action taken on things

01:58:12.360 --> 01:58:13.833
like the spacers?

01:58:16.050 --> 01:58:17.160
- [Penny] Duncan?

01:58:17.160 --> 01:58:19.650
- Yeah, so I would, just
to build on the point

01:58:19.650 --> 01:58:21.450
about engagement from staff,

01:58:21.450 --> 01:58:23.190
this is one of the things particularly

01:58:23.190 --> 01:58:27.360
that really heavily motivates
nurses and midwives,

01:58:27.360 --> 01:58:29.430
particularly if you see
that generational shift

01:58:29.430 --> 01:58:31.800
where we've got a much more
climate aware generation

01:58:31.800 --> 01:58:34.100
in our workforce now and
it's certainly will be one

01:58:34.100 --> 01:58:36.270
of the strands that we're working
on through the development

01:58:36.270 --> 01:58:38.910
of the nursing, midwifery
strategy for England.

01:58:38.910 --> 01:58:41.220
I guess there's something
about the pace of adoption

01:58:41.220 --> 01:58:43.290
and are we doing enough
to drive some of the pace

01:58:43.290 --> 01:58:46.620
of really good examples that
are going on across trust

01:58:46.620 --> 01:58:49.260
and make sure that they
are adopted and are we,

01:58:49.260 --> 01:58:51.180
do we need to do more on
a kind of zero tolerance

01:58:51.180 --> 01:58:53.130
on stopping certain products for example,

01:58:53.130 --> 01:58:54.360
where we know we have an impact

01:58:54.360 --> 01:58:56.160
and we'll have an impact on cost?

01:58:56.160 --> 01:58:58.200
And then I guess the risks

01:58:58.200 --> 01:59:00.360
around some of the other developments.

01:59:00.360 --> 01:59:03.300
So my understanding is that AI

01:59:03.300 --> 01:59:05.940
and some of those changes
requires a big heavy input

01:59:05.940 --> 01:59:06.930
of power.

01:59:06.930 --> 01:59:10.227
And so I guess the risks
for the next five years

01:59:10.227 --> 01:59:14.010
and the work that we need to
do in terms of transformation,

01:59:14.010 --> 01:59:17.850
are we going to replace one
improvement with another kind

01:59:17.850 --> 01:59:19.713
of increase in carbon?

01:59:20.880 --> 01:59:22.230
- Ravi.

01:59:22.230 --> 01:59:24.150
- On similar lines, I
was struck by the fact

01:59:24.150 --> 01:59:28.440
that secondary care energy
bills have doubled since 2019

01:59:28.440 --> 01:59:30.003
to over 1.4 billion.

01:59:31.230 --> 01:59:33.600
Just made me wonder about
whether we are maxing

01:59:33.600 --> 01:59:35.880
out on all the energy efficiency

01:59:35.880 --> 01:59:38.220
and solar generation projects

01:59:38.220 --> 01:59:39.600
that could potentially reduce bills.

01:59:39.600 --> 01:59:41.130
It sounded like you're doing some

01:59:41.130 --> 01:59:43.080
through the public sector
decarbonization fund

01:59:43.080 --> 01:59:45.960
that I think is stopping plus GB Energy.

01:59:45.960 --> 01:59:47.760
But are we doing all of them

01:59:47.760 --> 01:59:50.430
or are we constrained by the
kind of finance available?

01:59:50.430 --> 01:59:51.263
'Cause if we are,

01:59:51.263 --> 01:59:54.180
if there is other MPV
positive stuff that is there,

01:59:54.180 --> 01:59:57.210
we should be able to look at
other ways of financing this

01:59:57.210 --> 01:59:58.043
and particularly thinking

01:59:58.043 --> 02:00:00.510
about the distribution
network operators actually

02:00:00.510 --> 02:00:02.626
as a potential way of doing that.

02:00:02.626 --> 02:00:03.459
- [Penny] Meghana.

02:00:03.459 --> 02:00:04.623
- Thank you.

02:00:04.623 --> 02:00:06.330
So not answering your question,

02:00:06.330 --> 02:00:08.850
just making a comment about more sort

02:00:08.850 --> 02:00:12.270
of clinical engagement
throughout the NHS actually

02:00:12.270 --> 02:00:14.220
because we are pushing on an open door

02:00:14.220 --> 02:00:16.740
and with my CEO hat on,

02:00:16.740 --> 02:00:20.130
I can tell you about the
difficulties experienced.

02:00:20.130 --> 02:00:22.890
So the first was measurement.

02:00:22.890 --> 02:00:26.700
People couldn't get through the detail

02:00:26.700 --> 02:00:31.470
to understand the conversion
from sort of pounds to carbon.

02:00:31.470 --> 02:00:32.760
So that was the first thing.

02:00:32.760 --> 02:00:34.920
So we actually did a pilot project

02:00:34.920 --> 02:00:39.600
with Harvard University's
Professor Kaplan and Ramona

02:00:39.600 --> 02:00:42.390
and we focused on the
hip replacement pathway

02:00:42.390 --> 02:00:44.400
to look at scope one, two,

02:00:44.400 --> 02:00:49.290
and three in terms of how
much carbon is accounted for,

02:00:49.290 --> 02:00:52.230
for the day of admission
to the day of discharge

02:00:52.230 --> 02:00:54.870
for the most commonly done operation.

02:00:54.870 --> 02:00:56.550
And that's now being rolled out,

02:00:56.550 --> 02:00:58.980
and as a result of that understanding

02:00:58.980 --> 02:01:01.650
and working with the manufacturer

02:01:01.650 --> 02:01:03.600
who produces the hip implant,

02:01:03.600 --> 02:01:05.910
so looking at supply chain as well,

02:01:05.910 --> 02:01:07.890
it was very easy to then focus on,

02:01:07.890 --> 02:01:09.450
well, how do we improve this?

02:01:09.450 --> 02:01:11.970
What steps do we need to do, you know,

02:01:11.970 --> 02:01:14.760
cycling to work or whether it's switching

02:01:14.760 --> 02:01:18.360
off the theatres overnight
or stopping the manufacturer,

02:01:18.360 --> 02:01:21.900
sending the implant to Germany
for a coat just to come back

02:01:21.900 --> 02:01:22.740
to England.

02:01:22.740 --> 02:01:25.050
So all those things are, you know,

02:01:25.050 --> 02:01:28.770
currently in practise and
people are really focused

02:01:28.770 --> 02:01:30.030
on delivering this.

02:01:30.030 --> 02:01:31.777
- Right. Glen.

02:01:31.777 --> 02:01:35.070
- In answer to your question
about access to capital

02:01:35.070 --> 02:01:38.430
and whether this is
constraining our progress,

02:01:38.430 --> 02:01:42.927
well, it is, Salix is
a good example of that,

02:01:42.927 --> 02:01:44.280
and as far as I'm aware,

02:01:44.280 --> 02:01:46.560
when the Salix funding becomes available,

02:01:46.560 --> 02:01:50.040
it switches on and then
within a few hours,

02:01:50.040 --> 02:01:53.280
everyone has, all the money's gone,

02:01:53.280 --> 02:01:55.020
and you can't bid anymore.

02:01:55.020 --> 02:01:57.960
So there's a load of Salix
related projects out there

02:01:57.960 --> 02:01:59.340
that have not been funded today.

02:01:59.340 --> 02:02:02.223
So what's the future of
Salix moving forward?

02:02:03.420 --> 02:02:07.080
- Okay, so I think great
bit is you've got lots

02:02:07.080 --> 02:02:10.680
of enthusiasm here, which is really good.

02:02:10.680 --> 02:02:14.100
I think quite a few of these
are in some ways more prompts

02:02:14.100 --> 02:02:15.000
for more work

02:02:15.000 --> 02:02:18.600
and more focus rather than
necessarily questions to answer.

02:02:18.600 --> 02:02:22.226
I would just add a couple of bits as well

02:02:22.226 --> 02:02:23.059
and then you can come back

02:02:23.059 --> 02:02:24.960
on how we take this forward really.

02:02:24.960 --> 02:02:27.750
So I think Sam's point on outpatients,

02:02:27.750 --> 02:02:31.080
I thought the report of
about five, six years ago,

02:02:31.080 --> 02:02:32.310
Royal College of Physicians did,

02:02:32.310 --> 02:02:35.523
5% of all cars on the road
are going to an outpatient.

02:02:36.750 --> 02:02:37.983
Staggering figure.

02:02:39.180 --> 02:02:40.800
Words to Sam's point, you know,

02:02:40.800 --> 02:02:43.773
is that a good use of those car journeys?

02:02:45.720 --> 02:02:50.670
I would add to Sam's bit on
inhalers, wider EpiPens have

02:02:50.670 --> 02:02:53.523
to be replaced in schools
every single year.

02:02:54.600 --> 02:02:56.610
Does everyone need to wear plastic gloves?

02:02:56.610 --> 02:02:58.410
Does everyone need to
wear a plastic apron?

02:02:58.410 --> 02:03:01.620
I've never seen what a
plastic apron does, can't,

02:03:01.620 --> 02:03:02.760
doesn't seem to me to anyway.

02:03:02.760 --> 02:03:05.403
But you know, the list goes
on and on and on and on.

02:03:06.870 --> 02:03:09.030
Should we have a proper review of those?

02:03:09.030 --> 02:03:11.490
Should we ask NICE to
look at the evidence-base

02:03:11.490 --> 02:03:13.020
behind some of these things and whether

02:03:13.020 --> 02:03:15.390
or not we really do need to
keep replacing everything

02:03:15.390 --> 02:03:18.633
'cause it costs a lot of money
and so it's the environment.

02:03:20.160 --> 02:03:22.650
For me the most staggering
figure which we know,

02:03:22.650 --> 02:03:26.280
but anyway, 27 million of
square metres of buildings.

02:03:26.280 --> 02:03:28.170
So yes, energy efficient use of them,

02:03:28.170 --> 02:03:33.120
but we don't need probably
whatever, quarter a third,

02:03:33.120 --> 02:03:34.830
even half of that space.

02:03:34.830 --> 02:03:37.530
So how we actually going
to exit buildings in order,

02:03:37.530 --> 02:03:39.750
and you know, using that as an argument

02:03:39.750 --> 02:03:42.840
for going greener would be good.

02:03:42.840 --> 02:03:44.690
And then I think really important is,

02:03:45.540 --> 02:03:47.910
which bit, Meghana is a
bit about doing deep dives,

02:03:47.910 --> 02:03:50.940
but even at the high
level figures one and two,

02:03:50.940 --> 02:03:52.170
you don't need to answer that now,

02:03:52.170 --> 02:03:55.263
but I couldn't align the
data across the two of them,

02:03:56.130 --> 02:03:57.420
so don't get into that now.

02:03:57.420 --> 02:03:59.130
But there's a bit about
sort of being very clear

02:03:59.130 --> 02:04:01.530
on where are there
opportunities to improve

02:04:01.530 --> 02:04:03.390
and therefore what can we do.

02:04:03.390 --> 02:04:05.163
So you've got lots of enthusiasm.

02:04:06.060 --> 02:04:08.250
Tell us what we can do next.

02:04:08.250 --> 02:04:11.460
- Sure. Thank you for enthusiasm.

02:04:11.460 --> 02:04:13.170
I'll try and bundle a
few of those together.

02:04:13.170 --> 02:04:17.100
So look, staff engagement,
clinical engagement is massive.

02:04:17.100 --> 02:04:18.060
Last time we surveyed,

02:04:18.060 --> 02:04:21.470
we knew nine in 10 of our
staff wanted a greener NHS.

02:04:21.470 --> 02:04:22.773
So we know that's there.

02:04:23.910 --> 02:04:26.520
In terms of focusing on clinical pathways

02:04:26.520 --> 02:04:28.470
and opportunities especially around reuse.

02:04:28.470 --> 02:04:30.173
We've partnered with GIRFT,

02:04:31.350 --> 02:04:33.183
Getting It Right First Time programme.

02:04:34.050 --> 02:04:35.940
We've done our first
greener deep dive there

02:04:35.940 --> 02:04:37.440
on bladder cancer pathway.

02:04:37.440 --> 02:04:39.480
We've seen that actually
whilst we save two

02:04:39.480 --> 02:04:42.360
and a half thousand tonnes
through that revised pathway,

02:04:42.360 --> 02:04:45.510
we're also reducing the
number of appointments,

02:04:45.510 --> 02:04:49.020
outpatient appointments, faster diagnosis,

02:04:49.020 --> 02:04:50.730
patients spending less time in hospitals.

02:04:50.730 --> 02:04:55.730
So there's mutually beneficial
points for both our programme

02:04:56.550 --> 02:04:58.625
and the wider NHS in bringing

02:04:58.625 --> 02:05:00.870
clinicians into this programme.

02:05:00.870 --> 02:05:02.640
In terms of some of the other points

02:05:02.640 --> 02:05:04.920
around patient travel, inhalers,

02:05:04.920 --> 02:05:07.470
we published our revised
green plan guidance earlier

02:05:07.470 --> 02:05:08.303
this year.

02:05:08.303 --> 02:05:09.780
It's for the first time sets out a lot

02:05:09.780 --> 02:05:14.160
of best practise about where
organisations such as Jim's

02:05:14.160 --> 02:05:17.070
and David's, they're referenced
I think in there in terms

02:05:17.070 --> 02:05:20.400
of taking action and actually
trying to replicate that.

02:05:20.400 --> 02:05:22.320
We know that there are some
high performing trusts.

02:05:22.320 --> 02:05:24.930
We know that there are some
who probably need to catch up,

02:05:24.930 --> 02:05:28.140
and it's trying to bring, bridge that gap.

02:05:28.140 --> 02:05:30.243
Just on the point of capital delivery,

02:05:31.440 --> 02:05:32.850
there's a lot we can do in our programme

02:05:32.850 --> 02:05:35.790
to reduce emissions
without spending money,

02:05:35.790 --> 02:05:38.176
but capital delivery particularly.

02:05:38.176 --> 02:05:40.020
And I think the paper talks
about heat going forward.

02:05:40.020 --> 02:05:41.190
So it talks about Salix

02:05:41.190 --> 02:05:43.680
and public sector decarbonization scheme.

02:05:43.680 --> 02:05:46.800
They are the same thing
that they're in high demand

02:05:46.800 --> 02:05:49.650
and actually perhaps our
biggest challenge in terms

02:05:49.650 --> 02:05:52.650
of staying on track over
to our interim targets

02:05:52.650 --> 02:05:54.450
and longer is actually finding a way

02:05:54.450 --> 02:05:57.990
to find financial models
and vehicles to be able

02:05:57.990 --> 02:06:00.180
to deliver some of that decarbonization.

02:06:00.180 --> 02:06:01.890
So there's a few challenges there

02:06:01.890 --> 02:06:06.633
which we'd love to take
further beyond this board.

02:06:07.950 --> 02:06:10.050
- Alright, great. Really
good conversation.

02:06:10.050 --> 02:06:14.103
Look forward to seeing
16% get to 30% next year.

02:06:15.960 --> 02:06:16.793
Good.

02:06:16.793 --> 02:06:18.720
Okay, let's move on to the next one,

02:06:18.720 --> 02:06:20.520
increasing research activity.

02:06:20.520 --> 02:06:24.450
So Lindsey, I think you're
going to do this. Yep.

02:06:24.450 --> 02:06:25.778
We good.

02:06:25.778 --> 02:06:27.480
- Thanks very much,
Chair. Thanks, everybody.

02:06:27.480 --> 02:06:29.653
So I'll take the paper as read.

02:06:29.653 --> 02:06:31.560
I just want to make a couple of points

02:06:31.560 --> 02:06:34.770
before I hand over to Alex,
who's my colleague from DHSC.

02:06:34.770 --> 02:06:38.322
We work very closely with
our colleagues in NIHR

02:06:38.322 --> 02:06:40.140
and DHSC on this agenda.

02:06:40.140 --> 02:06:43.320
The paper frames this particular
issue within the context

02:06:43.320 --> 02:06:45.150
of the life sciences sector plan,

02:06:45.150 --> 02:06:48.030
but we really do want to focus
the board's attention today

02:06:48.030 --> 02:06:49.860
on the setup of clinical trials.

02:06:49.860 --> 02:06:53.220
It's a real political focus at the moment.

02:06:53.220 --> 02:06:55.730
It's in both the life sciences sector plan

02:06:55.730 --> 02:06:56.610
and our 10-year health plan.

02:06:56.610 --> 02:06:59.160
But it was kind of stimulated
by the prime minister

02:06:59.160 --> 02:07:02.310
making a commitment in
April to move to set

02:07:02.310 --> 02:07:06.960
up of clinical trials in
organisations in 150 days or under.

02:07:06.960 --> 02:07:09.570
Unfortunately at the
moment we are only managing

02:07:09.570 --> 02:07:13.110
that in about 30% of studies
that are on the NIHR portfolio.

02:07:13.110 --> 02:07:15.630
So we have a long way to go.

02:07:15.630 --> 02:07:17.820
That said, there's a
significant amount of work

02:07:17.820 --> 02:07:22.110
that is ongoing working through
our colleagues in the sector

02:07:22.110 --> 02:07:25.530
through the UK clinical
research delivery programme

02:07:25.530 --> 02:07:27.540
and the paper sets out the
work that we're doing there

02:07:27.540 --> 02:07:28.950
to standardise, streamline,

02:07:28.950 --> 02:07:32.220
and remove duplication
across organisations.

02:07:32.220 --> 02:07:34.860
It also sets out some of the requirements

02:07:34.860 --> 02:07:37.945
that we put out to the
system following feedback

02:07:37.945 --> 02:07:42.310
Vin Diwakar wrote to
organisations in May asking

02:07:43.629 --> 02:07:45.000
to increase board visibility

02:07:45.000 --> 02:07:47.280
and board accountability relating to this.

02:07:47.280 --> 02:07:49.500
And I think both Alex and
I smiled to each other,

02:07:49.500 --> 02:07:52.410
Penny, when you said what
gets measured gets done.

02:07:52.410 --> 02:07:53.880
We would love to move this agenda

02:07:53.880 --> 02:07:55.170
to what gets measured gets done.

02:07:55.170 --> 02:07:57.030
But we do really need this board's support

02:07:57.030 --> 02:07:58.590
to make sure that we are measuring it

02:07:58.590 --> 02:08:01.530
and then we understand what is happening.

02:08:01.530 --> 02:08:03.930
So really what we would like
the board to think about,

02:08:03.930 --> 02:08:05.040
and give us a steer on today

02:08:05.040 --> 02:08:07.470
is what more could we do if
you think there are areas

02:08:07.470 --> 02:08:09.120
that we aren't addressing,

02:08:09.120 --> 02:08:12.480
and if there are areas like
using the planning guidance,

02:08:12.480 --> 02:08:15.390
et cetera, where we can
actually pull some levers,

02:08:15.390 --> 02:08:16.950
are they appropriate?

02:08:16.950 --> 02:08:19.170
I'll pass on to Alex who I know
wants to make a few comments

02:08:19.170 --> 02:08:20.003
from DHSC point of view.

02:08:20.003 --> 02:08:21.330
- Yeah. Thanks, Lindsey.

02:08:21.330 --> 02:08:23.430
And I just wanted to
bring sort of perspective

02:08:23.430 --> 02:08:26.490
from the ministers and the
government's perspective.

02:08:26.490 --> 02:08:28.320
I think just to focus on sort of vision

02:08:28.320 --> 02:08:30.930
and then why we want to do it and the asks

02:08:30.930 --> 02:08:33.390
that the government wants,
I mean from the vision,

02:08:33.390 --> 02:08:36.030
the vision really is that we
want research embedded as part

02:08:36.030 --> 02:08:40.260
of everyday care and we want
the NHS seen to be competitive

02:08:40.260 --> 02:08:44.820
for clinical research, both for
non-commercial and academic,

02:08:44.820 --> 02:08:46.860
but in particular, commercial research.

02:08:46.860 --> 02:08:48.780
And just to flag, you know,

02:08:48.780 --> 02:08:51.270
commercial clinical
trials is the lifeblood

02:08:51.270 --> 02:08:52.620
of the life science industry.

02:08:52.620 --> 02:08:55.080
And at the moment, that isn't working.

02:08:55.080 --> 02:08:56.340
Why we want to do it?

02:08:56.340 --> 02:08:57.270
We want to do it

02:08:57.270 --> 02:09:00.690
because we want to bring new
treatments to patients faster.

02:09:00.690 --> 02:09:03.480
We want your support and the workforce

02:09:03.480 --> 02:09:06.060
so that they can be more productive

02:09:06.060 --> 02:09:10.200
and we can free them up to do
tasks that in reality matter.

02:09:10.200 --> 02:09:13.440
I know the minister's
very concerned about this.

02:09:13.440 --> 02:09:16.447
In fact I spoke to the
minister yesterday and he said,

02:09:16.447 --> 02:09:18.150
"Alex, do you want me to
come along to the board

02:09:18.150 --> 02:09:21.510
to really emphasise this?"

02:09:21.510 --> 02:09:25.653
So it is a very high political
agenda item at the moment,

02:09:25.653 --> 02:09:27.300
just to reiterate that.

02:09:27.300 --> 02:09:30.150
I think the three key asks for me

02:09:30.150 --> 02:09:32.580
and from the minister
really are, first of all,

02:09:32.580 --> 02:09:34.830
can we really make sure
that research is embedded

02:09:34.830 --> 02:09:38.040
as fully as everyday care and
it's not seen as an add-on

02:09:38.040 --> 02:09:39.633
or separate from healthcare?

02:09:40.470 --> 02:09:44.550
Secondly, I think, reflecting
what Lindsey's just said,

02:09:44.550 --> 02:09:47.230
you know, if we can get NHS trust boards

02:09:47.230 --> 02:09:50.280
to actually measure the
150-day metric as well

02:09:50.280 --> 02:09:52.260
as the other key performance
indicators we've got,

02:09:52.260 --> 02:09:53.400
that will make sure

02:09:53.400 --> 02:09:57.540
that a performance in commercial
trials gets delivered.

02:09:57.540 --> 02:10:00.000
And then thirdly, we have set out a number

02:10:00.000 --> 02:10:04.020
of policy guidances and
recommendations essentially

02:10:04.020 --> 02:10:06.690
and if we can embed
those more effectively,

02:10:06.690 --> 02:10:11.690
we are really keen and I think
that from our perspective,

02:10:12.210 --> 02:10:14.250
that will really help
deliver some of this.

02:10:14.250 --> 02:10:16.440
So those are the three asks. Thanks.

02:10:16.440 --> 02:10:17.733
- Okay, thank you.

02:10:17.733 --> 02:10:22.733
I've got Sam, Mark, and David. Okay, Sam.

02:10:23.670 --> 02:10:26.190
- Yeah, so one of my hats
is an honorary professor

02:10:26.190 --> 02:10:28.470
of primary care at Queen Mary University.

02:10:28.470 --> 02:10:32.670
And when I see the change
in research opportunities,

02:10:32.670 --> 02:10:35.970
in other words, the capture
of the 2 million database

02:10:35.970 --> 02:10:38.760
in primary care, the discovery
project that connects it

02:10:38.760 --> 02:10:43.050
to the acute sector, the
opportunities are massive,

02:10:43.050 --> 02:10:45.630
but we still have a research base

02:10:45.630 --> 02:10:47.730
which very much focuses on hospital.

02:10:47.730 --> 02:10:50.910
We haven't managed to
shift it towards the system

02:10:50.910 --> 02:10:54.510
and it plays I think to
Robert's point earlier on,

02:10:54.510 --> 02:10:58.980
about the drug companies
and the risk of them exiting

02:10:58.980 --> 02:11:00.390
because of costs.

02:11:00.390 --> 02:11:02.310
Have we done enough

02:11:02.310 --> 02:11:05.400
to sell the research
opportunities particularly

02:11:05.400 --> 02:11:09.273
to the drug companies that
are unique in this country?

02:11:11.340 --> 02:11:12.720
- Okay. Mark.

02:11:12.720 --> 02:11:14.040
- So I had a few points.

02:11:14.040 --> 02:11:17.250
First, welcome the paper
and the conversation

02:11:17.250 --> 02:11:20.665
that we are having, really important area.

02:11:20.665 --> 02:11:21.498
There's a few things though.

02:11:21.498 --> 02:11:23.190
One, I was unsure reading the paper

02:11:23.190 --> 02:11:24.660
about what the delivery
vehicle's going to be

02:11:24.660 --> 02:11:26.220
for these priorities.

02:11:26.220 --> 02:11:28.140
It's a little bit mixed I think, you know,

02:11:28.140 --> 02:11:30.120
'cause regions of course will need

02:11:30.120 --> 02:11:32.400
to play a role at some
point in the future,

02:11:32.400 --> 02:11:34.213
but given the changes
going on at regional level,

02:11:34.213 --> 02:11:35.970
I think just putting our focus

02:11:35.970 --> 02:11:38.130
on strengthening the
capability and capacity

02:11:38.130 --> 02:11:41.010
at regional level I think will mean

02:11:41.010 --> 02:11:43.500
that we don't realise the pace
of change that we're going

02:11:43.500 --> 02:11:46.440
to need to see in this particular area.

02:11:46.440 --> 02:11:49.930
Final point was around cost recovery

02:11:51.390 --> 02:11:52.350
because it is important for us

02:11:52.350 --> 02:11:54.510
to make sure we're covering costs,

02:11:54.510 --> 02:11:56.490
but that will not give us the growth

02:11:56.490 --> 02:11:58.380
that we're going to need to
see for commercial research.

02:11:58.380 --> 02:11:59.213
And I think that's something

02:11:59.213 --> 02:12:00.150
that we just need to think through.

02:12:00.150 --> 02:12:02.820
Not be silly about investments,
but really thinking

02:12:02.820 --> 02:12:06.240
ahead about where we know
there's going to be likely growth

02:12:06.240 --> 02:12:09.660
in research activity and
making sure we have a model

02:12:09.660 --> 02:12:11.190
that's going to support the increase

02:12:11.190 --> 02:12:13.560
at the pace they have an
opportunity to do to go out.

02:12:13.560 --> 02:12:14.550
Thanks.

02:12:14.550 --> 02:12:15.540
- [Penny] David.

02:12:15.540 --> 02:12:18.420
- Yeah, I mean really welcome the paper,

02:12:18.420 --> 02:12:19.740
I suppose just to remind everybody

02:12:19.740 --> 02:12:20.940
and the figure keeps changing,

02:12:20.940 --> 02:12:25.380
but every pound invested as
an eight to 10 pounds return

02:12:25.380 --> 02:12:27.660
to Mark's point, it doesn't, to the UKPLC,

02:12:27.660 --> 02:12:29.310
it doesn't necessarily fall in the place

02:12:29.310 --> 02:12:32.280
where you spend the money,
which is part of the challenge.

02:12:32.280 --> 02:12:33.480
Just to Sam's point,

02:12:33.480 --> 02:12:36.210
I chair the North London
clinical research network,

02:12:36.210 --> 02:12:37.770
which we've done for 10 years.

02:12:37.770 --> 02:12:39.930
The fastest growing
research is in primary care

02:12:39.930 --> 02:12:40.950
in terms of number.

02:12:40.950 --> 02:12:42.210
So there's opportunity there,

02:12:42.210 --> 02:12:43.080
but you're absolutely right,

02:12:43.080 --> 02:12:45.090
we need to harness it
differently and think differently

02:12:45.090 --> 02:12:47.400
about how we approach it going forward.

02:12:47.400 --> 02:12:48.780
And I suppose two final things.

02:12:48.780 --> 02:12:52.197
One, there are still
organisations out there

02:12:52.197 --> 02:12:53.670
and I understand the challenge

02:12:53.670 --> 02:12:55.500
where they are not spending money

02:12:55.500 --> 02:12:58.050
on research allocation
to save money overall,

02:12:58.050 --> 02:13:00.450
even though the letter's
gone out from colleagues,

02:13:00.450 --> 02:13:02.040
even though we've tried to reinforce it.

02:13:02.040 --> 02:13:03.840
So we need to find a different route

02:13:03.840 --> 02:13:05.820
to make that message stronger.

02:13:05.820 --> 02:13:06.660
And very finally,

02:13:06.660 --> 02:13:08.790
I had a good conversation
with Lucy last week,

02:13:08.790 --> 02:13:12.030
Lucy Chappell, and I'll support
her with a shelf of trust

02:13:12.030 --> 02:13:14.250
to seeing whether we can
benchmark some opportunities

02:13:14.250 --> 02:13:17.310
to speed up some of this
150-day target work.

02:13:17.310 --> 02:13:20.490
- Okay, so just, 'cause it
falls within your remit, David,

02:13:20.490 --> 02:13:23.910
the question about should we
be measuring this and measuring

02:13:23.910 --> 02:13:26.640
and looking at that variation
across all providers,

02:13:26.640 --> 02:13:30.390
is that something we
could add to that master?

02:13:30.390 --> 02:13:34.290
- Yeah, I mean it will be
relevant to a proportion

02:13:34.290 --> 02:13:35.550
of providers, absolutely,

02:13:35.550 --> 02:13:38.580
and research active
providers do measure it,

02:13:38.580 --> 02:13:41.580
but probably even not as
frequently as they should do.

02:13:41.580 --> 02:13:44.130
Organisations like mine,
like some of them represented

02:13:44.130 --> 02:13:45.750
down the table should be looking at this

02:13:45.750 --> 02:13:47.370
as they are the four-hour access target

02:13:47.370 --> 02:13:49.230
to be honest if we are prioritising this.

02:13:49.230 --> 02:13:50.880
So yeah, we'll take that away,

02:13:50.880 --> 02:13:52.620
and think about how best we can do that.

02:13:52.620 --> 02:13:54.810
- Okay, great. Thank you.

02:13:54.810 --> 02:13:57.870
Okay, so I've got Robert,
I've got Elizabeth,

02:13:57.870 --> 02:14:00.123
I've got Meghana, I've got Louise.

02:14:01.140 --> 02:14:03.357
- Yeah, well, there's lots
of great stuff in this paper

02:14:03.357 --> 02:14:04.500
and it's very welcome,

02:14:04.500 --> 02:14:07.743
and congratulations on what's going on.

02:14:08.730 --> 02:14:10.560
I think picking up on a couple of things.

02:14:10.560 --> 02:14:12.570
So picking up on Sam's point

02:14:12.570 --> 02:14:14.880
and I think the health
data research service plan

02:14:14.880 --> 02:14:18.840
is brilliant, I assume that'll
include primary care data

02:14:18.840 --> 02:14:20.490
and this links on to open safely,

02:14:20.490 --> 02:14:22.050
which is on the May ,

02:14:22.050 --> 02:14:25.590
but we're not discussing it,
which is a great way to ensure

02:14:25.590 --> 02:14:28.170
that that data's really for you accessed.

02:14:28.170 --> 02:14:29.457
This is focused on phase three trials

02:14:29.457 --> 02:14:32.130
and I understand that
and it's very important,

02:14:32.130 --> 02:14:35.370
but if you think about seven
F and partnering with industry

02:14:35.370 --> 02:14:37.110
to drive growth and innovation,

02:14:37.110 --> 02:14:40.560
early phase trials I would say
are also extremely important.

02:14:40.560 --> 02:14:42.990
And I don't think I saw a single mention

02:14:42.990 --> 02:14:45.900
of the university in this paper.

02:14:45.900 --> 02:14:49.470
And if we want to drive innovation
and industry partnerships

02:14:49.470 --> 02:14:51.360
and everything else,
we need on the one hand

02:14:51.360 --> 02:14:54.330
to have patient data readily available.

02:14:54.330 --> 02:14:56.430
That's what industry wants.

02:14:56.430 --> 02:14:58.550
But we need university
research to be drawn in,

02:14:58.550 --> 02:15:00.960
and then we need life sciences clusters,

02:15:00.960 --> 02:15:03.060
that bring those three sectors together,

02:15:03.060 --> 02:15:05.370
which is what is going to drive some

02:15:05.370 --> 02:15:08.040
of those early phase trials
to drive novel therapies.

02:15:08.040 --> 02:15:09.150
Because as I'm sure you know,

02:15:09.150 --> 02:15:12.600
50% of the drugs in the pipeline
are not small molecules.

02:15:12.600 --> 02:15:15.210
They're advanced
therapies, biologic cells,

02:15:15.210 --> 02:15:16.500
and genes, and all that.

02:15:16.500 --> 02:15:21.500
And that requires these NHS
academia industry clusters

02:15:21.600 --> 02:15:22.803
to be really thriving.

02:15:24.090 --> 02:15:24.923
- [Penny] Elizabeth.

02:15:24.923 --> 02:15:27.900
- Yeah, in past, David said
what I was going to say just

02:15:27.900 --> 02:15:30.420
about the return on
investment being compelling,

02:15:30.420 --> 02:15:33.360
but not always the cashable savings arrive

02:15:33.360 --> 02:15:36.150
in the NHS so the
engagement can be muddied,

02:15:36.150 --> 02:15:39.210
but I had the pleasure of
meeting with Lindsey and Vin

02:15:39.210 --> 02:15:42.420
about this topic and what
I was also surprised by,

02:15:42.420 --> 02:15:46.713
is the amount of work
that actually goes on,

02:15:47.640 --> 02:15:49.350
but the lack of cost recovery,

02:15:49.350 --> 02:15:51.750
even though the providers
have actually done the work,

02:15:51.750 --> 02:15:55.260
they don't follow through
to have recover those costs

02:15:55.260 --> 02:15:58.890
from commercial contract research.

02:15:58.890 --> 02:16:01.860
So there is an opportunity
there for us in the sector

02:16:01.860 --> 02:16:04.870
and it's on our agenda for
the 9th of October when we,

02:16:04.870 --> 02:16:05.807
or the 8th of October, sorry,

02:16:05.807 --> 02:16:07.650
when we bring the CFOs together again,

02:16:07.650 --> 02:16:09.180
and it's gone out in a kind of a,

02:16:09.180 --> 02:16:10.680
these are the things we
should be looking at,

02:16:10.680 --> 02:16:12.570
and with Lindsey, we
are trying to identify

02:16:12.570 --> 02:16:14.940
which providers are doing the work,

02:16:14.940 --> 02:16:18.180
but not recovering the
income, which is surprising,

02:16:18.180 --> 02:16:20.010
which just makes me think it's not linked

02:16:20.010 --> 02:16:24.060
through to the board or
execs or something is odd

02:16:24.060 --> 02:16:28.004
that's going on 'cause
for us to follow up on.

02:16:28.004 --> 02:16:28.837
- Meghana.

02:16:28.837 --> 02:16:30.270
- Thank you. Thank you for the paper.

02:16:30.270 --> 02:16:32.040
So I completely agree that, you know,

02:16:32.040 --> 02:16:35.100
we have to make it clear that
research is not an add-on.

02:16:35.100 --> 02:16:38.160
It is part and parcel because
there's clear evidence

02:16:38.160 --> 02:16:40.170
that research active organisations

02:16:40.170 --> 02:16:42.300
have lower mortality rates

02:16:42.300 --> 02:16:45.120
and there's a lot of research
in primary care actually.

02:16:45.120 --> 02:16:48.840
So I would support adding the
metrics related to research

02:16:48.840 --> 02:16:51.450
to the board frameworks.

02:16:51.450 --> 02:16:54.150
The point is it's not
just about the time taken

02:16:54.150 --> 02:16:57.840
to set up trials, it should
also be about access to research

02:16:57.840 --> 02:17:00.900
for diverse populations
and what percentage

02:17:00.900 --> 02:17:02.700
of diverse populations that come

02:17:02.700 --> 02:17:07.700
to clinic have that
research, you know, exposure.

02:17:07.920 --> 02:17:08.850
That's the second thing.

02:17:08.850 --> 02:17:12.900
And third thing is standardising
pathways is really good.

02:17:12.900 --> 02:17:15.210
It'll cut a lot of duplication

02:17:15.210 --> 02:17:18.390
and number of days taken to set up.

02:17:18.390 --> 02:17:23.390
However, if you get
organisations to do a process map

02:17:23.760 --> 02:17:28.760
of that pathway, you will find
specific delays at key points

02:17:29.190 --> 02:17:31.500
and one of them being pharmacy.

02:17:31.500 --> 02:17:32.333
- Yeah.

02:17:32.333 --> 02:17:35.250
- And that's not because
they are not quick,

02:17:35.250 --> 02:17:37.860
it's because there are
resource implications.

02:17:37.860 --> 02:17:41.400
So I think working through
those challenges will be key,

02:17:41.400 --> 02:17:43.800
and there'll be different
at different organisations.

02:17:43.800 --> 02:17:46.383
But I know that pharmacy is a key issue.

02:17:48.360 --> 02:17:52.890
- So I think we also need a
kind of corresponding narrative

02:17:52.890 --> 02:17:54.690
for the public and for particular groups

02:17:54.690 --> 02:17:57.060
about the importance of
taking part in research

02:17:57.060 --> 02:17:59.640
and I don't think people
associate research

02:17:59.640 --> 02:18:00.840
with primary care for example.

02:18:00.840 --> 02:18:01.969
And you know,

02:18:01.969 --> 02:18:03.930
it's kind of how do you
change that expectation?

02:18:03.930 --> 02:18:06.150
Sorry, Sam.

02:18:06.150 --> 02:18:09.090
- Yeah, that links into
the app allowing you

02:18:09.090 --> 02:18:11.601
to participate and so on.

02:18:11.601 --> 02:18:12.723
Tom?

02:18:12.723 --> 02:18:15.630
- So I come from a position
of having tried and failed

02:18:15.630 --> 02:18:20.630
to set up a commercial
clinical trial in the NHS

02:18:20.910 --> 02:18:23.190
having some direct personal experience,

02:18:23.190 --> 02:18:26.430
I didn't see anything
in the paper that I saw

02:18:26.430 --> 02:18:28.920
as fundamentally changing the paradigm

02:18:28.920 --> 02:18:29.910
that we're currently in.

02:18:29.910 --> 02:18:32.040
I don't think there's any question

02:18:32.040 --> 02:18:34.650
that if you ask any leader in the NHS,

02:18:34.650 --> 02:18:36.720
they'll tell you that they
think research is important.

02:18:36.720 --> 02:18:38.370
So just telling people
that it's important.

02:18:38.370 --> 02:18:41.193
I don't think it's going to
particularly change anything.

02:18:42.960 --> 02:18:45.360
I think we need to have
a discussion on the table

02:18:45.360 --> 02:18:46.350
about two things.

02:18:46.350 --> 02:18:49.260
Firstly, some clearer incentives.

02:18:49.260 --> 02:18:51.510
So this point about cost recovery.

02:18:51.510 --> 02:18:52.343
At the moment,

02:18:52.343 --> 02:18:55.620
I think the mindset for
most NHS organisations

02:18:55.620 --> 02:18:58.740
is that this is yet another thing to do,

02:18:58.740 --> 02:19:01.110
and it will involve some cost as opposed

02:19:01.110 --> 02:19:04.110
to being an opportunity
to generate some income.

02:19:04.110 --> 02:19:06.270
So I think shifting from a,

02:19:06.270 --> 02:19:10.230
this is a cost centre to
this is an income opportunity

02:19:10.230 --> 02:19:13.200
is the most significant
shift that we can make,

02:19:13.200 --> 02:19:15.270
and we need to construct some incentives

02:19:15.270 --> 02:19:19.110
to actually make that shift occur.

02:19:19.110 --> 02:19:20.730
And that also means
that the incentives have

02:19:20.730 --> 02:19:23.130
to be understood in the wider system.

02:19:23.130 --> 02:19:25.320
The second thing, and I
think we should explore this

02:19:25.320 --> 02:19:27.240
with our partners,

02:19:27.240 --> 02:19:32.240
is that we currently approach
UK assets like UK Biobank

02:19:33.000 --> 02:19:37.410
and presumably HDRs on the same basis

02:19:37.410 --> 02:19:39.720
as kind of global public goods.

02:19:39.720 --> 02:19:41.430
So any researcher anywhere

02:19:41.430 --> 02:19:45.000
in the world can access UK Biobank.

02:19:45.000 --> 02:19:46.200
Given the kind of pullback

02:19:46.200 --> 02:19:49.320
that we are seeing from the
pharmaceutical industry,

02:19:49.320 --> 02:19:50.790
I wonder whether it's time

02:19:50.790 --> 02:19:54.150
to explore whether there
should be privileged access

02:19:54.150 --> 02:19:56.760
to those UK assets for those companies

02:19:56.760 --> 02:19:59.220
that are research active in the UK,

02:19:59.220 --> 02:20:01.050
because at the moment
we're making them available

02:20:01.050 --> 02:20:04.920
on a completely equal basis
to any company anywhere,

02:20:04.920 --> 02:20:08.843
regardless of whether they
contribute to UK life sciences

02:20:08.843 --> 02:20:11.880
or they don't contribute
to UK life sciences.

02:20:11.880 --> 02:20:14.613
I think we should explore
that as a possibility.

02:20:15.660 --> 02:20:16.493
- [Penny] Paul.

02:20:17.700 --> 02:20:22.080
- Just reflecting on this being a cost

02:20:22.080 --> 02:20:26.490
and not seen as an investment
which can get a return,

02:20:26.490 --> 02:20:28.530
I think in probably the last 15 years,

02:20:28.530 --> 02:20:32.070
many universities have made that shift

02:20:32.070 --> 02:20:35.700
where research was sort of a
hobby that some people took

02:20:35.700 --> 02:20:38.370
and was a cost 'cause it
kept away from teaching

02:20:38.370 --> 02:20:40.380
to the universities recognising

02:20:40.380 --> 02:20:42.720
that they can get some return from this.

02:20:42.720 --> 02:20:45.720
But that is a change in not just mindset,

02:20:45.720 --> 02:20:48.450
but a change in organisational pattern.

02:20:48.450 --> 02:20:51.333
And so I think we could go
to not just the universities

02:20:51.333 --> 02:20:53.280
that have done this well for 50 years,

02:20:53.280 --> 02:20:55.883
but universities that
learned how to do that change

02:20:56.820 --> 02:20:59.400
and to transfer that learning

02:20:59.400 --> 02:21:01.562
into the National Health Service

02:21:01.562 --> 02:21:04.800
'cause it's quite recent
in these institutions.

02:21:04.800 --> 02:21:07.860
- Okay, so this has turned
into a bit more of a workshop,

02:21:07.860 --> 02:21:09.010
which I think is great.

02:21:10.230 --> 02:21:11.190
You've got low,

02:21:11.190 --> 02:21:13.633
I think I've written
down quite a few of them.

02:21:13.633 --> 02:21:15.630
You will have done lots
of really good ideas,

02:21:15.630 --> 02:21:17.250
thoughts, inputs.

02:21:17.250 --> 02:21:20.520
I think we have agreed that
we need to measure this

02:21:20.520 --> 02:21:23.580
and I think that includes
measuring what happens,

02:21:23.580 --> 02:21:25.290
maybe some stuff around finances,

02:21:25.290 --> 02:21:28.323
certainly how much is done in
primary care, wider settings.

02:21:29.160 --> 02:21:32.490
We don't have time for you
to come respond to it now,

02:21:32.490 --> 02:21:34.680
but I think take all these things away,

02:21:34.680 --> 02:21:37.740
come back with almost a
beefed up version of this,

02:21:37.740 --> 02:21:40.710
including some choices
like Tom's put a few

02:21:40.710 --> 02:21:42.663
like provocative choices out there.

02:21:43.530 --> 02:21:46.650
There are a whole wider set of actions

02:21:46.650 --> 02:21:50.400
for NHS England in the
life sciences sector plan.

02:21:50.400 --> 02:21:53.940
So what I propose that we
do is we'll bring it back

02:21:53.940 --> 02:21:57.900
with all of the actions
and include this sort of,

02:21:57.900 --> 02:21:59.790
I guess set of wider things

02:21:59.790 --> 02:22:01.740
that people have raised across the table.

02:22:01.740 --> 02:22:03.270
But thank you for bringing that.

02:22:03.270 --> 02:22:04.103
- Thanks for everybody.

02:22:04.103 --> 02:22:05.670
- Very good discussion.

02:22:05.670 --> 02:22:08.640
Okay, I'm doing very badly on my timing.

02:22:08.640 --> 02:22:11.880
Next item is for us to
accept without discussion

02:22:11.880 --> 02:22:15.090
the improved access data and analytics.

02:22:15.090 --> 02:22:16.740
I do actually have quite
a few things I'd quite

02:22:16.740 --> 02:22:18.490
like to raise, but I won't do that.

02:22:20.070 --> 02:22:23.880
Are people comfortable to
accept that set of directions?

02:22:23.880 --> 02:22:25.410
And we might pick up separately some

02:22:25.410 --> 02:22:27.000
of the questions that come from it.

02:22:27.000 --> 02:22:29.710
Okay, so we have some
questions from the public

02:22:30.600 --> 02:22:35.600
which Jim and Duncan and
Glen are going to respond to.

02:22:35.700 --> 02:22:38.490
Duncan, do you want to go
first with your two questions?

02:22:38.490 --> 02:22:39.870
- Yeah, so the first one is around,

02:22:39.870 --> 02:22:42.430
a question around the Supreme Court ruling

02:22:44.430 --> 02:22:49.430
on biological sex,
which happened in April.

02:22:51.300 --> 02:22:53.940
And the question is around
what is NHS England doing

02:22:53.940 --> 02:22:54.933
about this?

02:22:55.962 --> 02:22:56.795
And I think just to say

02:22:56.795 --> 02:22:59.760
that we are reviewing
our relevant policies

02:22:59.760 --> 02:23:00.780
and procedures to make sure

02:23:00.780 --> 02:23:02.100
that that is aligned with the ruling,

02:23:02.100 --> 02:23:05.280
but we do need to wait for the EHRC,

02:23:05.280 --> 02:23:07.590
which is the Equality and
Human Rights Commission

02:23:07.590 --> 02:23:10.650
to update its code of practise
for services, functions,

02:23:10.650 --> 02:23:13.740
and associations, which will
set out the legal framework

02:23:13.740 --> 02:23:17.760
of how services are delivered
in line with the Equality Act.

02:23:17.760 --> 02:23:19.560
And we need that available

02:23:19.560 --> 02:23:23.400
before we can make final
decisions about national policy.

02:23:23.400 --> 02:23:26.460
So the updated code of practise
will be published shortly

02:23:26.460 --> 02:23:28.680
subject to parliamentary approval

02:23:28.680 --> 02:23:31.290
and we will therefore
once that has been done,

02:23:31.290 --> 02:23:34.710
update the guidance and publish that.

02:23:34.710 --> 02:23:38.160
So we are kind of waiting on
that timeline essentially.

02:23:38.160 --> 02:23:39.420
And then there was a question, Penny,

02:23:39.420 --> 02:23:42.510
about the graduate guarantee
for nurses and midwives,

02:23:42.510 --> 02:23:45.750
which was launched in August this year,

02:23:45.750 --> 02:23:49.920
recognising that some
student nurses and midwives

02:23:49.920 --> 02:23:54.330
that were qualifying this year
were struggling to find jobs.

02:23:54.330 --> 02:23:56.910
There was, the government
announced the graduate guarantee,

02:23:56.910 --> 02:23:59.580
which essentially we are working on,

02:23:59.580 --> 02:24:02.190
and working the regional teams

02:24:02.190 --> 02:24:05.370
and across trusts have been
really leaning into this

02:24:05.370 --> 02:24:08.190
to make sure that we
support student nurses

02:24:08.190 --> 02:24:11.580
and midwives to find
roles not just in the NHS,

02:24:11.580 --> 02:24:12.420
but more broadly

02:24:12.420 --> 02:24:14.370
'cause there aren't just
vacancies in the NHS.

02:24:14.370 --> 02:24:16.530
There are vacancies across social care,

02:24:16.530 --> 02:24:19.620
the independent sector, and other places.

02:24:19.620 --> 02:24:23.730
And so we are trust as supporting measures

02:24:23.730 --> 02:24:25.650
about recruiting ahead of turnover,

02:24:25.650 --> 02:24:27.900
which we know happens every year,

02:24:27.900 --> 02:24:30.390
making sure that they're
using vacant roles creatively.

02:24:30.390 --> 02:24:32.340
And we've also made a million pounds worth

02:24:32.340 --> 02:24:35.340
of funding available
specifically for midwifery

02:24:35.340 --> 02:24:38.370
to convert temporarily some
maternity support worker roles

02:24:38.370 --> 02:24:41.550
which are vacant into midwifery roles.

02:24:41.550 --> 02:24:42.690
The guarantee is for this year,

02:24:42.690 --> 02:24:43.830
clearly there is work that's going

02:24:43.830 --> 02:24:47.820
on on the new 10-year workforce plan

02:24:47.820 --> 02:24:49.470
and the nursing midwifery strategy,

02:24:49.470 --> 02:24:51.780
which will look at future years.

02:24:51.780 --> 02:24:55.890
- Okay, thank you. Jim, voluntary
redundancy for ICB staff.

02:24:55.890 --> 02:24:58.470
- Yeah, we touched on
this briefly earlier.

02:24:58.470 --> 02:25:01.440
So a few weeks ago we met
with new cluster chairs

02:25:01.440 --> 02:25:03.780
and chief execs and talked
through where we are,

02:25:03.780 --> 02:25:07.320
where the potential is for a plan B

02:25:07.320 --> 02:25:09.870
if we don't reach agreement
with the treasury.

02:25:09.870 --> 02:25:12.570
We are actively in discussion,
as I've said earlier,

02:25:12.570 --> 02:25:16.620
with treasury and hopefully
we will resolve that soon.

02:25:16.620 --> 02:25:19.773
But if not, we'll have
to revert to plan B.

02:25:20.760 --> 02:25:23.005
That's probably two, three weeks time.

02:25:23.005 --> 02:25:24.360
I think we'll have to draw a line,

02:25:24.360 --> 02:25:27.060
and then at that point we'll engage again.

02:25:27.060 --> 02:25:30.150
Every ICB has a plan that they can enact.

02:25:30.150 --> 02:25:32.370
It is limited by the
resource that they've got,

02:25:32.370 --> 02:25:34.320
so it would be more of a stage process.

02:25:34.320 --> 02:25:39.320
The benefits would be less
and slower than anticipated.

02:25:40.230 --> 02:25:43.770
But we will activate that as
soon as we've reached a point

02:25:43.770 --> 02:25:45.480
of either concluding the discussions

02:25:45.480 --> 02:25:47.880
or needing to revert to plan B.

02:25:47.880 --> 02:25:48.750
- Okay, thank you.

02:25:48.750 --> 02:25:52.050
And Glen, questions about
regional differences

02:25:52.050 --> 02:25:54.150
in terms of how vacancies are managed.

02:25:54.150 --> 02:25:57.510
- Yeah, so links to the question
Duncan's already answered

02:25:57.510 --> 02:26:01.590
about the graduate guarantee
and who does what question

02:26:01.590 --> 02:26:04.550
in the new framework of the NHS.

02:26:04.550 --> 02:26:08.530
So we set out in the
model region blueprint

02:26:09.390 --> 02:26:10.890
on the eighth of September

02:26:10.890 --> 02:26:11.877
and that's the first version of that.

02:26:11.877 --> 02:26:13.773
And we'll do an updated version.

02:26:14.700 --> 02:26:16.530
That's a high level mandate

02:26:16.530 --> 02:26:19.440
which sets out the
functional roles of regions,

02:26:19.440 --> 02:26:23.340
but also is to ensure
consistency across the regions,

02:26:23.340 --> 02:26:25.290
particularly on this issue.

02:26:25.290 --> 02:26:27.480
They will take the role of
strategic workforce planning

02:26:27.480 --> 02:26:30.990
at regional level and as part
of the oversight framework,

02:26:30.990 --> 02:26:32.820
we will be monitoring the performance

02:26:32.820 --> 02:26:36.570
of individual provider
organisations, but also ICBs.

02:26:36.570 --> 02:26:39.510
We will do a further
update of model region

02:26:39.510 --> 02:26:42.030
when we've completed the
10-year workforce plan,

02:26:42.030 --> 02:26:44.970
which will reinforce some
of those responsibilities.

02:26:44.970 --> 02:26:46.440
- Okay, thank you.

02:26:46.440 --> 02:26:49.383
Does anyone have any other
business they'd like to raise?

02:26:50.700 --> 02:26:53.100
Okay, well, thank you all very much.

02:26:53.100 --> 02:26:56.290
I think you've been extremely
broad ranging conversation

02:26:57.210 --> 02:27:00.840
and we will take forward the
various actions highlighted

02:27:00.840 --> 02:27:02.520
and look forward to the next time.

02:27:02.520 --> 02:27:03.353
Thank you.

